window.COURSE={"title":"Chess Academy · Mastery Course","version":"2.0.0","modules":[{"name":"Chess vision & safety","subtitle":"Think clearly, see threats, prevent blunders","ids":[1,4,17,18,19],"icon":"♔","color":"green"},{"name":"Tactical patterns","subtitle":"Spot forcing ideas and calculate their consequences","ids":[2,3,20,21,22,23,24],"icon":"♘","color":"gold"},{"name":"Opening principles","subtitle":"Develop with purpose, protect the king, play the center","ids":[5,6,25,26],"icon":"♙","color":"blue"},{"name":"Opening laboratory","subtitle":"Compare options before choosing a repertoire","ids":[7,8,9,10,11,12,27,28,29,30],"icon":"♗","color":"lavender"},{"name":"Middlegame strategy","subtitle":"Build plans, improve pieces, and create pawn breaks","ids":[13,14,31,32,33,34],"icon":"♜","color":"pink"},{"name":"Endgame technique","subtitle":"Convert advantages and defend difficult endings","ids":[15,35,36,37,38],"icon":"♚","color":"teal"},{"name":"Practical improvement","subtitle":"Analyze, review, and transfer knowledge into games","ids":[16,39,40],"icon":"♛","color":"gold"}],"lessons":[{"id":1,"title":"A thinking routine that prevents blunders","body":["Before searching for a clever idea, ask a practical question: What did the opponent’s last move threaten? At the 800–1000 level, recognizing a loose piece or an immediate check is often more valuable than remembering an obscure opening variation.","Use a consistent scan: (1) Is my king safe? (2) What checks do either of us have? (3) What captures change the position? (4) What forcing threats are available? (5) Which of my pieces is unprotected or attacked more times than defended?","Then examine two or three candidate moves. For each, picture the opponent’s strongest reply before committing. A move can obey an opening principle and still lose material to a tactic."],"takeaways":["Identify the opponent’s threat.","Check forcing moves before quiet moves.","After choosing, imagine the strongest reply."],"target":"Use checks, captures, threats and opponent-threat inspection before choosing a move.","worked":"","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["e2e4","White stakes a claim in the center and frees the king’s bishop."],["e7e5","Black contests e4 and claims equal central space."],["g1f3","White develops with a concrete threat to the e5 pawn."],["b8c6","Black defends e5 while developing a knight."],["f1c4","The bishop develops actively and bears down on f7."],["f8c5","Black develops and puts comparable pressure on f2."]],"exercises":[{"id":"V01-a1","lesson":1,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After your opponent moves, what should you identify before choosing your response?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Their immediate threats","Your favorite opening name","The move you planned five turns ago","Whether the clock is green"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Scan the changed threats first.","hint":"Think about opponent intent."},{"id":"V01-a2","lesson":1,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which search sequence often finds tactics efficiently?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Checks, captures and threats","Only quiet pawn moves","Only queen moves","Only king moves"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Forcing moves constrain the response tree.","hint":"Consider moves needing immediate answers."}],"module":0,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["V01-a1","V01-a2"]},{"id":2,"title":"Forks and double attacks","body":["A fork is a single move that creates two or more attacks. Knights are famous for forks because their L-shaped moves can attack a king and a rook or queen at once. Pawns, queens, rooks and bishops can also make double attacks.","Do not assume every fork wins. A fork only matters if the forked targets cannot escape together and the attacking piece survives long enough to benefit. Always ask: Can the opponent capture the forking piece, give check, or create a stronger threat?","In our composed example, a knight can jump into c7 with check, attacking the black king on e8 and rook on a8. The check forces a response; afterward the rook is vulnerable."],"takeaways":["Look for knight jumps near kings and major pieces.","Calculate the response to the check.","Do not confuse “attacks two things” with “wins two things.”"],"target":"Recognize one move that attacks two targets, and check whether the attacker can be captured.","worked":"Find Nc7+ in the diagram. It checks the black king and attacks the rook. Notice how a forcing move can win time.","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"r3k3/8/8/1N6/8/8/8/4K3 w - - 0 1","line":[["b5c7","Knight c7 gives check AND attacks the rook on a8."]],"exercises":[{"id":"P01","lesson":2,"kind":"board","prompt":"Find the knight fork that checks Black and attacks the rook on a8.","fen":"r3k3/8/8/1N6/8/8/8/4K3 w - - 0 1","answer":"b5c7","explanation":"A knight from b5 jumps to c7 with check, also attacking a8.","hint":"Look for a knight check that attacks a second target."},{"id":"P16","lesson":2,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After the fork Nc7+, which valuable Black piece is attacked alongside the king?","fen":"r3k3/8/8/1N6/8/8/8/4K3 w - - 0 1","answer":0,"explanation":"The knight on c7 attacks the rook on a8 as well as the king on e8.","hint":"Trace the knight’s possible jumps from c7.","options":["The rook on a8.","The queen on d8.","The bishop on c8.","The rook on h8."]},{"id":"V02-a1","lesson":2,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why may a knight fork fail despite attacking two pieces?","fen":"r3k3/8/8/1N6/8/8/8/4K3 w - - 0 1","options":["The knight may be captured or an enemy check may intervene","Knights cannot fork rooks","Forked pieces always escape","Forks count as illegal moves"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Calculate whether the forking piece survives.","hint":"Inspect the reply."}],"module":1,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P01","P16","V02-a1"]},{"id":3,"title":"Pins, skewers and discovered checks","body":["A pin happens when moving a front piece would expose a more valuable piece behind it. An absolute pin exposes the king, so the pinned piece cannot legally leave the line. A relative pin exposes a queen or other material: moving is legal, but often costly.","A skewer reverses the order: a more valuable piece is attacked first and must usually move, exposing a less valuable piece behind it. A discovered attack occurs when one piece moves away and reveals an attack from a rook, bishop, or queen.","Double check is a special form of discovered check in which two pieces check the king at once. The checked side must move the king; blocking or capturing only one checking piece cannot solve both checks."],"takeaways":["Name the front and rear targets on every line.","For a discovered check, identify both checking lines.","A pin against the queen is not the same as an illegal move."],"target":"Separate three related line-piece motifs and calculate their consequences.","worked":"Here the knight is relatively pinned: moving away can expose the queen to the bishop. This is not an absolute pin because the piece behind it is a queen, not the king.","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"4k3/8/8/8/6b1/5N2/8/3QK3 w - - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"P02","lesson":3,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why is White’s knight on f3 relatively pinned?","fen":"4k3/8/8/8/6b1/5N2/8/3QK3 w - - 0 1","answer":1,"explanation":"The bishop on g4 attacks the queen on d1 through the knight. Moving it is legal but may lose the queen.","hint":"Trace the diagonal behind the knight.","options":["It is illegal for White to move the knight, because the king would be checked.","The bishop points through the knight toward White’s queen.","The knight is pinned by Black’s queen to White’s rook.","The knight can move, but doing so always loses the king."]},{"id":"P03","lesson":3,"kind":"board","prompt":"Find the rook check that skewers the Black king and queen.","fen":"q7/8/k7/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","answer":"e1a1","explanation":"Ra1+ forces Black to resolve check, leaving the queen on a8 exposed.","hint":"A rook wants to line up with the king and queen."},{"id":"P04","lesson":3,"kind":"board","prompt":"Find the bishop move that gives double check.","fen":"4k3/8/8/8/4B3/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","answer":"e4g6","explanation":"Bg6+ opens the rook’s e-file attack AND attacks the king from g6.","hint":"Moving the bishop also uncovers a rook attack."},{"id":"V03-a1","lesson":3,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What distinguishes an absolute pin from a relative pin?","fen":"4k3/8/8/8/6b1/5N2/8/3QK3 w - - 0 1","options":["An absolute pin exposes the king","A relative pin is always illegal","An absolute pin needs a queen","Pins only involve knights"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The king may not be left in check, so an absolute pin restricts legal moves.","hint":"Which piece is behind the pinned one?"}],"module":1,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P02","P03","P04","V03-a1"]},{"id":4,"title":"King safety and mating patterns","body":["A king with friendly pawns on f7, g7 and h7 (for Black) can be trapped on the back rank. If an enemy rook reaches that rank with check, the king may have no escape square. A back-rank mate requires checking whether the defender can capture the rook, block the line, or respond with another legal move.","When you are defending, consider a timely luft move such as ...h6 or h3 (depending on color and position) to create an escape square. Do not push pawns automatically: pawn moves can also create weaknesses.","Learn the geometry first, then solve mixed tactical positions so you no longer know the theme in advance. This mirrors the progression from pattern recognition to calculation."],"takeaways":["Does the king have a flight square?","Can the rook be captured or the check blocked?","Before pushing a pawn, consider new weaknesses."],"target":"Recognize why back-rank mates work, and how to create an escape square.","worked":"Rxe8# works in the diagram: the white rook captures the opposing rook and gives mate along the eighth rank, while Black’s own pawns block the king’s exits.","refs":["S09","S11"],"fen":"4r1k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","line":[["e1e8","Rxe8# uses the trapped king’s back rank."]],"exercises":[{"id":"P05","lesson":4,"kind":"board","prompt":"White to move: find checkmate in one.","fen":"4r1k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","answer":"e1e8","explanation":"Rxe8# is mate: the king has no flight square, block, or useful capture.","hint":"Look at the e-file and the trapped back rank."},{"id":"V04-a1","lesson":4,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When can making a quiet developing move be dangerous?","fen":"4r1k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","options":["When an immediate forcing threat goes unanswered","When you control the center","When you have castled","When queens are on the board"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Meeting tactical threats takes priority over general improvement.","hint":"Threats come before plans."},{"id":"V04-a2","lesson":4,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which question belongs in a safe-move checklist?","fen":"4r1k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","options":["What is my opponent’s strongest reply?","Can I move a rook early regardless of the position?","Can I ignore checks to build space?","Can I stop my opponent from thinking?"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Calculate a credible reply to your candidate move.","hint":"Picture the opponent’s turn."}],"module":0,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P05","V04-a1","V04-a2"]},{"id":5,"title":"Opening priorities, not opening commandments","body":["Opening theory is the body of tested plans and move sequences for reaching playable middlegames. Beginners need its ideas before its long variations: fight for e4, d4, e5 and d5; develop pieces to active squares; safeguard the king; and avoid losing time or material.","A useful rule of thumb is to bring minor pieces into play before repeatedly moving the queen or the same piece. But this is not a law: a forced recapture, a tactical opportunity or a response to a threat is more important than a slogan.","The practical goal is not to “win the opening” on move six. It is to reach a middlegame with coordinated pieces, a reasonably safe king, and a plan. After move ten, compare your development and king safety with your opponent’s, as a documented opening study plan recommends."],"takeaways":["Who controls the center?","Which minor pieces remain on original squares?","Which king can castle safely?"],"target":"Explain central control, development, king safety, and tempo in plain language.","worked":"White’s knight on f3 develops with a threat to e5; the bishop on c4 points toward f7. Black’s knight c6 defends e5. Every move has a purpose.","refs":["S03","S05","S11"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["e2e4","White stakes a claim in the center and frees the king’s bishop."],["e7e5","Black contests e4 and claims equal central space."],["g1f3","White develops with a concrete threat to the e5 pawn."],["b8c6","Black defends e5 while developing a knight."],["f1c4","The bishop develops actively and bears down on f7."],["f8c5","Black develops and puts comparable pressure on f2."],["c2c3","White prepares a later d4 central break."],["g8f6","Black develops while attacking White’s e4 pawn."]],"exercises":[{"id":"P06","lesson":5,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What are the two main jobs of 3.Bc4 in the Italian Game?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","e7e5","g1f3","b8c6","f1c4"]},"answer":1,"explanation":"The bishop develops actively and bears down on f7, a sensitive square early in the game.","hint":"Consider both piece activity and the attacked diagonal.","options":["It defends the queen and attacks the a8 rook.","It develops the bishop and creates pressure against f7.","It immediately forces checkmate on f7.","It prevents Black from castling on either side."]},{"id":"V05-a1","lesson":5,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why should knights and bishops usually develop early?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["They become useful on active squares","They immediately promote","They make pawn moves illegal","They prevent all checks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Developed pieces influence key squares.","hint":"Think piece mobility."},{"id":"V05-a2","lesson":5,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the point of castling in many openings?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Improve king safety and connect a rook toward play","Win a pawn automatically","Check the opponent","Force a draw"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Castling relocates king and rook with one move.","hint":"What happens to two pieces?"}],"module":2,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P06","V05-a1","V05-a2"]},{"id":6,"title":"Diagnose an opening after move ten","body":["Use a four-part opening scorecard: center, development, king safety and immediate threats. Do not give yourself a “good opening” just because you followed memorized moves; look at the position you reached.","Count undeveloped knights and bishops. Ask whether any pawn is attacked without enough defenders and whether your king is stuck in the center while files are opening. Finally, identify one useful next move: improve a piece, support the center, or castle.","Measuring opening success this way helps when opponents leave your prepared lines. Opening understanding is the ability to handle unfamiliar replies, not to force the opponent into your favorite variation."],"takeaways":["Can I name one concrete weakness?","Can I explain my next two useful moves?","What would my opponent like to play?"],"target":"Make a simple scorecard rather than judging only by material.","worked":"In this model, both sides have some development and king safety. White can consider Re1 and Nbd2, aiming for a later central expansion; this is a plan, not a forced line.","refs":["S03","S04","S05"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["e2e4","White stakes a claim in the center and frees the king’s bishop."],["e7e5","Black contests e4 and claims equal central space."],["g1f3","White develops with a concrete threat to the e5 pawn."],["b8c6","Black defends e5 while developing a knight."],["f1c4","The bishop develops actively and bears down on f7."],["f8c5","Black develops and puts comparable pressure on f2."],["c2c3","White prepares a later d4 central break."],["g8f6","Black develops while attacking White’s e4 pawn."],["d2d3","White reinforces e4 and chooses a quieter center."],["d7d6","Black supports e5 and frees the light-squared bishop."],["e1g1","White castles before beginning major central operations."],["e8g8","Black castles. Both sides are ready for middlegame plans."]],"exercises":[{"id":"P23","lesson":6,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent plays an unfamiliar move on move four. What should you do first?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","answer":0,"explanation":"Inspect the threat, examine forcing moves, then choose a sound developing or central response.","hint":"Avoid assuming the opponent must follow your prepared line.","options":["Find their threat, look for checks and captures, then choose a safe useful move.","Continue memorized moves regardless of threats.","Trade queens immediately to avoid calculations.","Always move the same developed piece again."]},{"id":"V06-a1","lesson":6,"kind":"mc","prompt":"If your opponent departs from a memorized line, what matters most?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Respond to the actual threats and position","Play the next memorized move automatically","Resign immediately","Trade your queen as soon as possible"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Opening principles are guides, not forced scripts.","hint":"Follow the current board."},{"id":"V06-a2","lesson":6,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What should you avoid when learning openings?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Memorizing without understanding plans","Studying central pawn breaks","Studying typical pawn structures","Checking king safety"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plans transfer to unfamiliar move orders.","hint":"Look for transferable knowledge."}],"module":2,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P23","V06-a1","V06-a2"]},{"id":7,"title":"Italian Game: the quiet foundation","body":["The Italian Game begins 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4. It is a classical king-pawn opening that combines natural development with tactical pressure against f7. White’s bishop on c4 attacks along the diagonal toward that pawn; in the initial position f7 is defended only by Black’s king.","After 3...Bc5, the Giuoco Piano family has many playable paths. For a first repertoire, the calm setup with c3, d3, O-O, Re1 and Nbd2 is easier to understand than memorizing a sharp gambit. Your long-term central idea is often d4 when it is well prepared.","Do not copy the plan against every move without thinking. If Black threatens e4, you must defend or calculate the pawn. If Black strikes the center, reassess before developing automatically."],"takeaways":["Memorize 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4.","Understand why c3 and d3 serve different purposes.","Castling is a strategic goal, not an excuse to ignore tactics."],"target":"Learn the first three moves and one strategic setup for White.","worked":"The model position is symmetrical. White has a solid center and may develop Re1 and Nbd2. Black can answer with their own useful development. Neither side is “winning by theory.”","refs":["S05","S03"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["e2e4","White stakes a claim in the center and frees the king’s bishop."],["e7e5","Black contests e4 and claims equal central space."],["g1f3","White develops with a concrete threat to the e5 pawn."],["b8c6","Black defends e5 while developing a knight."],["f1c4","The bishop develops actively and bears down on f7."],["f8c5","Black develops and puts comparable pressure on f2."],["c2c3","White prepares a later d4 central break."],["g8f6","Black develops while attacking White’s e4 pawn."],["d2d3","White reinforces e4 and chooses a quieter center."],["d7d6","Black supports e5 and frees the light-squared bishop."],["e1g1","White castles before beginning major central operations."],["e8g8","Black castles. Both sides are ready for middlegame plans."]],"exercises":[{"id":"P07","lesson":7,"kind":"board","prompt":"In this Italian Game position, which White pawn move prepares the central d4 advance?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","e7e5","g1f3","b8c6","f1c4","f8c5"]},"answer":"c2c3","explanation":"c3 supports d4 and leaves White a useful central expansion plan.","hint":"Which pawn can help the d-pawn advance safely?"},{"id":"V07-a1","lesson":7,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why does Bc4 in the Italian Game aim at f7?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It targets a pawn initially defended only by Black’s king","It pins Black’s queen to the rook","It gives check immediately","It traps White’s own knight"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The f7 square is sensitive before Black completes development.","hint":"Look at the bishop diagonal."},{"id":"V07-a2","lesson":7,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What does White often prepare with c3?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The central d4 pawn break","A kingside pawn promotion","Castling queenside immediately","A perpetual check"],"answer":0,"explanation":"c3 can support d4.","hint":"Think of the pawn on d2."}],"module":3,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P07","V07-a1","V07-a2"]},{"id":8,"title":"Italian Game: central break and open lines","body":["In the Italian, c3 can support d4. The break d4 challenges Black’s central pawn on e5, may attack a bishop on c5, and may open files and diagonals after exchanges. The cost is time and a more complicated position.","Consider the illustrative continuation 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Bc5 4.c3 Nf6 5.d4 exd4 6.cxd4 Bb4+ 7.Nc3. This shows how a central advance can lead immediately to a check and the need for concrete calculation.","A playable plan is not a guarantee of advantage. When you consider d4, count defenders of e4 and d4, look for ...exd4, and ask whether Black has a tactical reply such as ...Bb4+."],"takeaways":["Before d4, identify Black’s capturing replies.","Notice checks created by opening the center.","Accept that opening lines branch, rather than following one script."],"target":"Understand when c3 and d4 increase White’s activity.","worked":"The move 7.Nc3 blocks the bishop’s check while developing a knight. The resulting position is active and tactical; it requires more calculation than the quiet d3 plan.","refs":["S05"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["e2e4","Central control and a bishop diagonal."],["e7e5","Black meets the central claim."],["g1f3","Knight attacks e5."],["b8c6","Knight defends e5."],["f1c4","Bishop eyes f7."],["f8c5","Black points toward f2."],["c2c3","The c-pawn supports a d4 advance."],["g8f6","Black attacks e4."],["d2d4","White challenges e5 and attacks the bishop on c5 with the pawn."],["e5d4","Black captures the advancing d-pawn."],["c3d4","White recaptures and gains central space."],["c5b4","Black checks along the diagonal toward e1."],["b1c3","White blocks check and develops a knight."]],"exercises":[{"id":"P08","lesson":8,"kind":"board","prompt":"White to move: find the central pawn break that challenges e5 and the bishop on c5.","fen":{"after":["e2e4","e7e5","g1f3","b8c6","f1c4","f8c5","c2c3","g8f6"]},"answer":"d2d4","explanation":"d4 attacks the bishop on c5 and challenges Black’s e5 center. ...exd4 is a main reply.","hint":"The c-pawn already supports an advance of the d-pawn."},{"id":"V08-a1","lesson":8,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What should you calculate after playing d4 in an Italian structure?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The exchanges after ...exd4","Whether White’s rook is on a8","Whether c1 is attacked by a knight","Whether the game has ended"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Central breaks often force pawn exchanges.","hint":"Imagine your pawn being captured."},{"id":"V08-a2","lesson":8,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why may an early d4 increase tactical complexity?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It opens central lines","It makes castling illegal","It prevents checks","It promotes the pawn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Open lines help active pieces and create tactical pressure.","hint":"Think about opening diagonals."}],"module":3,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P08","V08-a1","V08-a2"]},{"id":9,"title":"Italian Game: attacking f7 with care","body":["After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6, Black attacks e4 and the position is called the Two Knights Defense. One sharp White choice is 4.Ng5, aiming at f7. Black has a principled defense with 4...d5. After 5.exd5 the position is already theory-rich.","An important practical lesson: pressure on f7 does not automatically mean an immediate winning capture. If you study a famous tactical pattern, learn the defender’s best resource. In the Two Knights, the line with ...Na5 differs significantly from ...Nxd5, so memorizing a one-move trap is not enough.","For your current level, the quiet 4.d3 is also a perfectly usable practical choice. Keep both ideas in your toolkit: 4.d3 to develop calmly, and 4.Ng5 only when prepared to calculate the critical center response."],"takeaways":["When one side attacks f7, look for central counterplay.","Know what ...d5 changes in the position.","Choose a calm line when you cannot yet calculate a sharp one."],"target":"Recognize the Two Knights position and distinguish an idea from a trap.","worked":"The point is not that White has already won: Black must respond accurately. Compare 5...Na5 (a principal defensive approach) with the tempting 5...Nxd5?! (riskier due to tactical ideas).","refs":["S05","S09"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["e2e4","White claims the center."],["e7e5","Black responds symmetrically."],["g1f3","White attacks e5."],["b8c6","Black defends e5."],["f1c4","White aims along the f7 diagonal."],["g8f6","Two Knights Defense; Black attacks e4."],["f3g5","White presses f7, committing to a sharp approach."],["d7d5","Black immediately counters in the center."],["e4d5","White captures on d5; the theory branches here."]],"exercises":[{"id":"P09","lesson":9,"kind":"board","prompt":"Black just played ...Nf6 in the Two Knights Defense. What quiet move can White use to protect e4?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","e7e5","g1f3","b8c6","f1c4","g8f6"]},"answer":"d2d3","explanation":"d3 supports e4 and keeps the position quieter than 4.Ng5.","hint":"A central pawn can reinforce e4."},{"id":"P20","lesson":9,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4, which Black replies lead to Giuoco Piano and Two Knights respectively?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","e7e5","g1f3","b8c6","f1c4"]},"answer":0,"explanation":"3...Bc5 is Giuoco Piano; 3...Nf6 is Two Knights Defense, attacking e4.","hint":"One response develops the bishop, the other the knight.","options":["3...Bc5 = Giuoco Piano; 3...Nf6 = Two Knights.","3...Bc5 = Two Knights; 3...Nf6 = Giuoco Piano.","3...d6 = Giuoco Piano; 3...Bc5 = Two Knights.","3...Nf6 = Giuoco Piano; 3...a6 = Two Knights."]},{"id":"V09-a1","lesson":9,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the defining move of the Two Knights Defense after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["3...Nf6","3...h5","3...a5","3...g5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Black develops the g8 knight and attacks e4.","hint":"Which knight can challenge e4?"}],"module":3,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P09","P20","V09-a1"]},{"id":10,"title":"Caro-Kann: why ...c6 supports ...d5","body":["The Caro-Kann Defense begins 1.e4 c6. Black prepares ...d5, directly challenging White’s central e4 pawn. Unlike a French Defense setup with an early ...e6, the Caro-Kann often lets the light-squared bishop develop outside the pawn chain before ...e6.","A standard Classical line is 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Bf5 5.Ng3 Bg6. Here ...Bf5 is not a random bishop move: Black develops while attacking the knight on e4. White gains tempos against the bishop in some variations, so Black must know retreat squares and not blindly chase pawns.","Your practical priority with Black is to complete development, preserve a healthy center, and find an appropriate pawn break. The Caro-Kann can be solid but is not tactically automatic or always easy."],"takeaways":["After 1.e4, answer 1...c6 and plan ...d5.","In the Classical, understand ...dxe4, ...Bf5, ...Bg6.","Stay alert to kingside expansion by White."],"target":"Build a Black repertoire around the central challenge and active bishop.","worked":"White has more central space and may attack the bishop. Black has exchanged the central e-pawn and developed the light-squared bishop. Learn the trade-off, not just the sequence.","refs":["S06","S12"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["e2e4","White opens with the king’s pawn."],["c7c6","Black prepares ...d5 with pawn support."],["d2d4","White builds a broad center."],["d7d5","Black challenges the e4 pawn."],["b1c3","White develops and protects e4."],["d5e4","Black captures the e4 pawn."],["c3e4","White recaptures with the knight."],["c8f5","Black develops the bishop outside the pawn chain, attacking e4."],["e4g3","White attacks the bishop with the knight."],["f5g6","Black retreats, preserving the bishop."]],"exercises":[{"id":"P10","lesson":10,"kind":"board","prompt":"Black to move: choose a useful development square for the light-squared bishop in the Classical Caro-Kann.","fen":{"after":["e2e4","c7c6","d2d4","d7d5","b1c3","d5e4","c3e4"]},"answer":"c8f5","explanation":"...Bf5 develops the bishop actively before ...e6, attacking the knight on e4.","hint":"Develop the c8 bishop outside the pawn chain."},{"id":"V10-a1","lesson":10,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why does the Caro-Kann begin with ...c6?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["To support ...d5","To check the king immediately","To prevent White from castling","To win e4 without moving again"],"answer":0,"explanation":"...c6 underpins the central ...d5 thrust.","hint":"Look at which pawn c6 can defend."},{"id":"V10-a2","lesson":10,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why might Black play ...Bf5 before ...e6?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["To develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain","To checkmate immediately","To castle on the queenside automatically","To pin the a1 rook"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The bishop can develop before ...e6 closes its diagonal.","hint":"Look at c8 bishop freedom."}],"module":3,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P10","V10-a1","V10-a2"]},{"id":11,"title":"Caro-Kann: Advance and Exchange choices","body":["White may play 3.e5 rather than exchange. In the Advance Variation (1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.e5), White gains space, while Black generally looks for pressure against the pawn center. A typical development choice is 3...Bf5, followed later by ...e6 and a carefully timed ...c5.","White may instead choose 3.exd5, the Exchange Variation. Black usually answers ...cxd5, recapturing toward the center and developing normally. An example is 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.Bd3 Nc6 5.c3 Nf6 6.Bf4.","Your decision tree can stay small: against 3.Nc3 or 3.Nd2, learn the Classical response; against 3.e5, understand ...Bf5 and pressure on d4; against 3.exd5, recapture with the c-pawn and develop. Other sidelines exist and need later expansion."],"takeaways":["Advance: White’s e5 pawn gains space.","Exchange: the c-pawn often recaptures on d5.","Plans matter more than memorizing every sideline."],"target":"Recognize two common deviations from the Classical line.","worked":"Black’s c-pawn has advanced from c6 to c5, challenging the base of White’s pawn chain at d4. The best timing depends on the position; this is a model plan.","refs":["S06"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["e2e4","White begins with e4."],["c7c6","Black starts the Caro-Kann."],["d2d4","White builds a pawn center."],["d7d5","Black challenges it."],["e4e5","Advance Variation: White gains space."],["c8f5","Black gets the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain."],["g1f3","White develops and protects central squares."],["e7e6","Black supports the center and creates a compact pawn structure."]],"exercises":[{"id":"P11","lesson":11,"kind":"board","prompt":"Black to move: where can the bishop develop effectively in the Caro-Kann Advance?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","c7c6","d2d4","d7d5","e4e5"]},"answer":"c8f5","explanation":"...Bf5 is a standard developing choice before ...e6 closes the light bishop’s path.","hint":"The light bishop wants an active square before ...e6."},{"id":"P12","lesson":11,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Exchange Caro-Kann, 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5, which Black pawn recaptured on d5?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","c7c6","d2d4","d7d5","e4d5","c6d5"]},"answer":2,"explanation":"The pawn from c6 captured diagonally on d5, keeping a central pawn and opening the c-file.","hint":"Follow the diagonal capture ...cxd5.","options":["The pawn on e7.","The pawn on d7.","The pawn on c6.","The pawn on f7."]},{"id":"P21","lesson":11,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Caro-Kann Advance 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.e5, what is White’s pawn-chain base and a common Black break?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","c7c6","d2d4","d7d5","e4e5"]},"answer":0,"explanation":"White’s chain base is d4; Black often challenges it with ...c5.","hint":"A chain’s base is the pawn nearer its own side.","options":["d4, and ...c5.","e5, and ...f6.","d4, and ...h5.","e4, and ...b5."]},{"id":"V11-a1","lesson":11,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Advance Caro-Kann, what is a common pawn break for Black?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["...c5 against d4","...h5 against h2","...a5 against a2","...g5 on the first move"],"answer":0,"explanation":"...c5 challenges the base of White’s central pawn chain.","hint":"Which pawn supports e5?"}],"module":3,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P11","P12","P21","V11-a1"]},{"id":12,"title":"Queen’s Gambit Declined: the central skeleton","body":["The Queen’s Gambit Declined (QGD) begins 1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6. White uses the c-pawn to challenge Black’s central d5 pawn. Black declines the gambit by supporting d5 with e6 instead of taking on c4.","A classical Orthodox setup runs 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.Bg5 Be7 5.e3 O-O 6.Nf3 Nbd7. Black is solid, gets the king safe, and retains the central d5 pawn; the light-squared bishop on c8 can take more work to activate.","Black frequently seeks a later ...c5 pawn break to attack White’s d4 pawn. Learn the strategic relationship: White presses in the center and can gain space; Black resists and looks for a freeing break. This is not a rigid response to every 1.d4 system."],"takeaways":["Identify why ...e6 defends d5.","Notice the c8 bishop’s development problem.","Know that ...c5 is a common idea, not a move to force instantly."],"target":"Understand why 1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6 is a respected Black setup.","worked":"Black has castled and developed both kingside pieces; the c8 bishop remains behind its e6 pawn. A common long-term goal is freeing play with ...c5.","refs":["S07","S08","S13"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["d2d4","White occupies the center."],["d7d5","Black responds in the center."],["c2c4","Queen’s Gambit: White pressures d5 with the c-pawn."],["e7e6","Black defends d5, declining to take c4."],["b1c3","White develops and increases central pressure."],["g8f6","Black develops and contests e4."],["c1g5","White pins the knight on f6 to Black’s queen."],["f8e7","Black prepares safe castling and breaks the pin’s potential."],["e2e3","White opens the bishop’s diagonal and reinforces d4."],["e8g8","Black castles kingside."],["g1f3","White completes another step in development."],["b8d7","Black develops while retaining a solid center."]],"exercises":[{"id":"P13","lesson":12,"kind":"board","prompt":"Black to move: find a move that reinforces the d5 pawn in the Queen’s Gambit.","fen":{"after":["d2d4","d7d5","c2c4"]},"answer":"e7e6","explanation":"...e6 defends d5; the position is the Queen’s Gambit Declined.","hint":"The e-pawn can reinforce d5."},{"id":"P14","lesson":12,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In many Queen’s Gambit Declined setups, what is a drawback of an early ...e6?","fen":{"after":["d2d4","d7d5","c2c4","e7e6","b1c3","g8f6","c1g5","f8e7","e2e3","e8g8","g1f3","b8d7"]},"answer":3,"explanation":"...e6 can restrict the bishop on c8. Black often solves the problem through careful development or pawn breaks.","hint":"Consider the diagonal from c8 toward h3.","options":["It blocks the queen’s usual path to d8.","It removes Black’s right to castle.","It places the knight on b8 in check.","It restricts the bishop on c8."]},{"id":"P22","lesson":12,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Queen’s Gambit Declined, what pawn break often challenges White’s d4 center?","fen":{"after":["d2d4","d7d5","c2c4","e7e6"]},"answer":1,"explanation":"...c5 is a common break that contests the d4 pawn and the center. The exact timing matters.","hint":"Look at the c-pawn’s role in challenging d4.","options":["...h6 to attack the king immediately.","...c5 to challenge White’s central pawns.","...a6 to win the queen on d1.","...g5 to prevent White from castling."]},{"id":"V12-a1","lesson":12,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the definition of the QGD after 1.d4 d5 2.c4?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["2...e6","2...h6","2...g5","2...b5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"...e6 reinforces the central pawn on d5.","hint":"Which pawn can defend d5?"}],"module":3,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P13","P14","P22","V12-a1"]},{"id":13,"title":"A practical middlegame planning method","body":["When you leave your prepared opening, do not panic. List the imbalances: king safety, central control, material, pawn weaknesses, open files, active pieces and vulnerable squares. You do not need an elaborate master-level evaluation; choose the two factors that matter most.","If a piece is badly placed, improve it. If the center is stable, prepare a pawn break. If your opponent has a loose piece, consider whether a forcing tactical move exists. If your king is unsafe, repairing that problem usually takes priority.","A practical question for every move is “What changes after this move?” For example, in the quiet Italian, Re1 may support e4 and prepare a later d4; in the QGD, ...c5 can contest White’s pawn center. Neither is always best without checking the concrete position."],"takeaways":["Name a worst-placed piece on each side.","Look for pawn breaks and open files.","Check tactics before carrying out a positional plan."],"target":"Translate a position into one clear, testable plan.","worked":"From Black’s side, identify the developed minor pieces and the remaining c8 bishop. Ask whether a later ...c5 or ...e5 break is feasible and what White threatens in response.","refs":["S01","S03","S13"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["d2d4","White occupies the center."],["d7d5","Black responds in the center."],["c2c4","Queen’s Gambit: White pressures d5 with the c-pawn."],["e7e6","Black defends d5, declining to take c4."],["b1c3","White develops and increases central pressure."],["g8f6","Black develops and contests e4."],["c1g5","White pins the knight on f6 to Black’s queen."],["f8e7","Black prepares safe castling and breaks the pin’s potential."],["e2e3","White opens the bishop’s diagonal and reinforces d4."],["e8g8","Black castles kingside."],["g1f3","White completes another step in development."],["b8d7","Black develops while retaining a solid center."]],"exercises":[{"id":"V13-a1","lesson":13,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a sensible starting point for a middlegame plan?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Identify your worst-placed piece and key weaknesses","Decide to sacrifice regardless of calculation","Move a random pawn","Copy an unrelated game"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plans respond to the actual position and piece activity.","hint":"Which piece needs help?"},{"id":"V13-a2","lesson":13,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why should a plan be reevaluated?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The opponent changes the position each move","You should never change a plan","It must last ten moves","Only engine moves can change plans"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The opponent’s threats and structural changes affect your priorities.","hint":"Consider new threats."}],"module":4,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["V13-a1","V13-a2"]},{"id":14,"title":"Calculation and comparing candidate moves","body":["Try a three-stage method: first list two or three plausible candidate moves; second analyze checks, captures and direct threats in each line; third compare the resulting positions. Avoid stopping at “my move threatens the queen” when your opponent can answer with checkmate.","When you solve a puzzle, write what your opponent would do after your move. If your analysis relies on the opponent making a blunder, look for a stronger defense. A correct pattern noticed for the wrong reason is not yet reliable knowledge.","In real games, you will not have perfect engine certainty. Your goal is disciplined thinking. Keep a small error log: missed checks, hanging pieces, calculation too shallow, or opening move forgotten. Make next week’s drills respond to those errors.","Chess players say a move **gains time** when it creates a threat your opponent must answer while also helping your own position. For instance, moving a rook to give check can force the king to move, allowing the rook to capture a valuable piece on the next move. This does not add time to the clock."],"takeaways":["Analyze the opponent’s most forcing reply.","Stop calculating only when the position is quiet enough to evaluate.","Record the cause of errors, not just the engine move."],"target":"Calculate forcing replies before selecting your favorite move.","worked":"In this composed position, Ra1+ skewers the black king in front of the queen. The critical idea is the forced king response, after which the rook can take the queen if no stronger defense intervenes.","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"q7/8/k7/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","line":[["e1a1","The rook gives check; Black’s queen lies behind the king."]],"exercises":[{"id":"P15","lesson":14,"kind":"board","prompt":"White to move: find a rook move that checks Black's king on a6. After the king escapes, the rook can capture Black's queen on a8.","fen":"q7/8/k7/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","answer":"e1a1","explanation":"Ra1+ checks the king along the a-file. Black must respond to check, and White can then take the queen on a8. A move that creates an urgent threat while improving your position is sometimes called 'gaining time' (or gaining a tempo).","hint":"Move your rook to a square where it attacks the king vertically. Look at the queen behind the king."},{"id":"P17","lesson":14,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why can Black not block only the rook’s e-file check after Bg6+?","fen":"4k3/8/8/8/4B3/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","answer":1,"explanation":"It is double check: both bishop and rook attack the king. Black must move the king.","hint":"Two different lines are delivering check.","options":["Because a bishop can always move twice on a turn.","Because both the bishop and rook are checking, so Black must move the king.","Because the queen cannot block rook checks under chess rules.","Because the bishop pins Black’s queen to the rook."]},{"id":"V14-a1","lesson":14,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When calculating, whose strongest response do you need?","fen":"q7/8/k7/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","options":["Your opponent’s","Only your own","Nobody’s until game review","Only the opening book’s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A line works only if it survives the best defense.","hint":"Think about resisting an attack."}],"module":4,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P15","P17","V14-a1"]},{"id":15,"title":"King and pawn endings: opposition and pawn races","body":["In king-and-pawn endgames, your king can help your pawns advance and block the other king. **Opposition** means the two kings face each other on the same row or column with exactly one empty square between them. Because kings may not stand next to each other, the king that must move first often has to step aside.","Imagine White's king on e4 and Black's king on e6. Square e5 lies between them. If it is Black's turn and Black has no useful pawn move, Black must move its king, potentially letting White advance. This is called White having the opposition. It helps control space, but pawns and exact legal moves decide whether anyone wins.","In a pawn race, count the moves to promotion and whether an enemy king can catch the pawn. The 'square rule' is a shortcut for estimating that chase. Try the position on the board before checking the answer."],"takeaways":["Opposition: kings face each other with one square between them.","The king forced to move first may have to give way.","Pawns and other moves still decide the outcome."],"target":"Understand the active king, opposition, and the square rule.","worked":"The kings in the example stand on e4 and e6, with e5 between them. Black is to move and must either move its king or play a pawn move. Because pawns are present, look at those moves too: opposition alone does not prove a win.","refs":["S01","S11"],"fen":"8/7p/4k3/8/4K3/8/P7/8 b - - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"P18","lesson":15,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Black to move: the kings face each other on e4 and e6, with one empty square between them. What does Black have to do, and does this decide who wins?","fen":"8/7p/4k3/8/4K3/8/P7/8 b - - 0 1","answer":0,"explanation":"The two kings face each other across e5, and Black must move first. This is called White having the opposition. Opposition may help White gain space, but the pawns and legal moves still determine the result.","hint":"Black is to move. Can a player skip a turn? Which king must leave its square?","options":["Black must move the king away from e6, but that alone does not determine the winner.","White must move its king immediately, so Black wins.","Black can leave its king on e6 and pass its turn.","White automatically wins, no matter where the pawns are."]},{"id":"P19","lesson":15,"kind":"mc","prompt":"White’s pawn stands on e5 and Black’s king on a8. Without other help, can the king catch the pawn?","fen":"k7/8/8/4P3/8/8/8/4K3 w - - 0 1","answer":1,"explanation":"No. White needs only e6, e7, e8=Q; Black’s king starts too far away to catch it.","hint":"Count pawn moves to promotion against the king’s route.","options":["Yes, the king controls e8 from a8.","No, the king is too far away.","Yes, because kings can move diagonally.","No, because the pawn has already promoted."]},{"id":"V15-a1","lesson":15,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In a king-and-pawn endgame, how can you use two kings facing each other with one empty square between them (called 'opposition')?","fen":"8/7p/4k3/8/4K3/8/P7/8 b - - 0 1","options":["Make the other king step aside so your king may enter useful squares.","Make your king move twice in a row.","Stop either player from moving their pawns.","Guarantee a win even if the other player can promote a pawn."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Opposition is about whose king must move away first. It often helps restrict the opposing king and gain access to useful squares. It does not guarantee a win.","hint":"Think about the fact that kings cannot stand next to each other."}],"module":5,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P18","P19","V15-a1"]},{"id":16,"title":"How to study and review your real games","body":["Play at a time control that permits deliberate thought, such as 15+10 when you have time. Immediately afterward, mark three moments: the first move you did not understand, the first significant tactical mistake, and one moment you struggled to choose a plan.","Analyze without an engine for a few minutes. Write a candidate move and the response you feared. Only then compare with computer analysis. An engine can identify tactical flaws, but you still need to explain the idea in ordinary words.","Use spaced retrieval for openings: play the line from memory the next day, again after several days, then again the following week. When you forget a move, revisit the position and the underlying plan. A short, maintained repertoire is better than a huge set of lines you cannot use."],"takeaways":["For every game record one opening lesson, one tactical lesson, one planning lesson.","Use an engine to check your explanation, not replace it.","Revisit missed exercises on a longer schedule."],"target":"Build a repeatable improvement loop rather than guessing what to study.","worked":"","refs":["S03","S04","S10"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["e2e4","White stakes a claim in the center and frees the king’s bishop."],["e7e5","Black contests e4 and claims equal central space."],["g1f3","White develops with a concrete threat to the e5 pawn."],["b8c6","Black defends e5 while developing a knight."],["f1c4","The bishop develops actively and bears down on f7."],["f8c5","Black develops and puts comparable pressure on f2."],["c2c3","White prepares a later d4 central break."],["g8f6","Black develops while attacking White’s e4 pawn."],["d2d3","White reinforces e4 and chooses a quieter center."],["d7d6","Black supports e5 and frees the light-squared bishop."]],"exercises":[{"id":"P24","lesson":16,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After losing a rapid game in the opening, which review notes are most helpful before turning on an engine?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","answer":1,"explanation":"Record the first surprise, a missed threat or tactical mistake, and what you considered playing. Then consult the engine.","hint":"The best notes preserve your reasoning rather than just the result.","options":["Only the final engine evaluation.","The first unfamiliar move, a missed threat, and your candidate plan.","The number of clicks and the time spent moving the queen.","Every legal move in the game, without comments."]},{"id":"V16-a1","lesson":16,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What should game review produce beyond an engine score?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["One specific habit to practice next time","A guarantee of a win","A list of every possible legal move","A new rating made by the engine"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A specific corrective habit makes analysis transferable.","hint":"Think about the next game."},{"id":"V16-a2","lesson":16,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When should you turn on the engine in a self-review workflow?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["After writing down your ideas and confusions","Before looking at the position","Only after winning","Never, because engines have no use"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Your thoughts provide learning context for engine evaluations.","hint":"Keep your decision record."}],"module":6,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Based on the original Volume 1 chapter; full reference links on the Sources page.","exerciseIds":["P24","V16-a1","V16-a2"]},{"id":17,"title":"Loose pieces and attacker counting","body":["An unprotected piece is a tactical target, even when it is not immediately attacked. Before making a move, list which pieces your opponent can capture and what guards each one.","When a piece is attacked more times than it is defended, count the *order* of captures, not just the number of attackers. Kings, pins and intermediate moves can invalidate a simple count.","A useful scan at every move is “What became loose after the last move?”"],"takeaways":["Find loose pieces","Count attackers and defenders","Check the order of captures"],"target":"Notice undefended pieces and calculate exchanges before offering a trade.","worked":"","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V17-1","lesson":17,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A knight is attacked once and protected by no pieces. What is the immediate concern?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It may be captured without recapture","It automatically gives check","It must move to the edge","It prevents castling"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If the attacker can legally capture, the undefended knight can be lost.","hint":"Think about recaptures."},{"id":"V17-2","lesson":17,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When is counting attackers and defenders alone not enough?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["When a square is central","When a defending piece is pinned","When both players castled","When both queens remain"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pinned defenders may be unable to recapture, so count legal forcing replies.","hint":"Ask whether every defender can actually move."},{"id":"V17-t","lesson":17,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Before attacking a queen, your rook is left unprotected. Which habit should you use?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Scan your loose pieces first","Always ignore your rook","Capture your own rook","Only count pawn moves"],"answer":0,"explanation":"You need to consider loose material before starting your attack.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":0,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V17-1","V17-2","V17-t"]},{"id":18,"title":"Candidate moves and blunder checks","body":["Before choosing a move, first identify the opponent’s immediate threats. Then compare two or three candidate moves rather than automatically playing your first idea.","Look at checks, captures, and threats for both sides. At every candidate, ask what the opponent could play immediately.","This simple habit prevents many one-move blunders without requiring deep calculation."],"takeaways":["Identify threat","Find alternatives","Check strongest reply"],"target":"Use a short threat-and-reply checklist before every important move.","worked":"","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V18-1","lesson":18,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Before you play a queen move, what should you check first?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Whether it looks aggressive","Whether the opponent has a forcing reply","Whether it matches a famous opening","Whether you can move the same queen again"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Considering checks, captures and threats against your queen and king catches many blunders.","hint":"Think about the opponent’s turn."},{"id":"V18-2","lesson":18,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why compare at least two candidate moves?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The first legal move is always best","It makes the clock stop","An alternative may meet the same goal more safely","It prevents a draw by repetition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Alternatives reveal tactical problems and help you find safer improvements.","hint":"Your first idea is a hypothesis."},{"id":"V18-t","lesson":18,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your first move looks promising, but you see a counter-check. What next?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Calculate the forcing counter-check","Play immediately anyway","Resign","Ignore king safety"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A forcing counter-check can change your preferred move.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":0,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V18-1","V18-2","V18-t"]},{"id":19,"title":"Threats, forcing moves, and safe defense","body":["Forcing moves reduce an opponent’s choices. Checks, captures, and serious threats should be inspected before quiet plans.","Defending is an active skill. Sometimes the best defense is to capture the attacker, trade a key piece, or create a stronger counter-threat.","Do not confuse a threat with an actual tactic: calculate the opponent’s best defense before assuming you win."],"takeaways":["Checks first","Consider captures","Verify counter-threats"],"target":"Find forcing candidate moves and a response to an immediate attack.","worked":"","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V19-1","lesson":19,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which action must be answered immediately under chess rules?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A pawn push","A discovered attack on a rook","A check to the king","A bishop developing to an open diagonal"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A player in check must legally remove the check.","hint":"One of these restricts legal replies."},{"id":"V19-2","lesson":19,"kind":"mc","prompt":"If your queen is attacked, which could be a valid response?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Capturing the attacker safely","Ignoring it to play an unrelated pawn move","Leaving the queen en prise always","Claiming a draw immediately"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A safe capture of the attacker can remove the threat without retreating.","hint":"Look for defense by forcing action."},{"id":"V19-t","lesson":19,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A side has an immediate capture but is also in check. What comes first?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Find a legal way out of check","Capture regardless","Offer a pawn","Make a neutral developing move"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Leaving the king in check is illegal.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":0,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V19-1","V19-2","V19-t"]},{"id":20,"title":"Back-rank mates and king escape squares","body":["A back-rank mate appears when a king cannot escape its own last rank, often because friendly pawns occupy its escape squares.","A rook or queen can deliver the finishing check along that rank. Before attacking, check whether the defender has a flight square or can interpose.","When defending, creating a safe escape square with h3 or h6 can help, but pawn moves must be tactically sound."],"takeaways":["Look for trapped king","Find a flight square","Verify checks and blocks"],"target":"Recognize back-rank mating conditions and practical defenses.","worked":"","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V20-1","lesson":20,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A king is trapped behind its own pawns on the back row. When can an enemy rook or queen check be especially dangerous?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["When the king has no safe square to escape to.","When both bishops have already been traded.","When the attacker has two pawns on the same file.","When the queen moved before move five."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A back-rank mate occurs when a rook or queen checks a king trapped on its home row with no escape square.","hint":"Think about where the king could move after a check."},{"id":"V20-2","lesson":20,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which is a possible *preventive* idea against a back-rank mate?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Open a safe flight square for your king","Move every pawn toward the enemy king","Always sacrifice the rook","Refuse to castle in all positions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Creating luft can give the king an escape, provided the pawn move is safe.","hint":"Think about king mobility."},{"id":"V20-t","lesson":20,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A king has a safe escape square. Is every back-rank rook check mate?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["No; verify all king replies","Yes; any rook check is mate","Yes; pawns cannot move","Only in blitz"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An available safe flight square may refute the mating claim.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":1,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V20-1","V20-2","V20-t"]},{"id":21,"title":"Removing defenders and overloading","body":["A defender may protect a king, piece, or critical square. If you remove that defender through capture or a forcing attack, the protected target can become vulnerable.","An overloaded piece has two important defensive jobs and may be unable to perform both. Count which threats occur first.","These tactics work only when your intended follow-up is legal and forcing enough; do not sacrifice automatically just to remove a guard."],"takeaways":["Identify key defender","Look for overload","Calculate follow-up"],"target":"Find how defensive duties create tactical targets.","worked":"","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V21-1","lesson":21,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A rook is protecting a bishop and also stopping checkmate. What is the danger?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The rook may not be able to defend both threats at once (it is overloaded).","The rook has automatically been promoted.","The game is already a stalemate.","The rook can no longer move."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An overloaded piece has too many important defensive jobs. The attacker may force it to abandon one of them.","hint":"Imagine the rook is forced to defend one threat. What happens to the other?"},{"id":"V21-2","lesson":21,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why is capturing a defender sometimes useful?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It gives your opponent an extra piece","It opens a route to its protected target","It always wins instantly","It changes which side moves"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Eliminating the guard can expose a more valuable piece or critical mating square.","hint":"Focus on the defended target."},{"id":"V21-t","lesson":21,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A key defender is exchanged, exposing a hanging queen. What made the exchange useful?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It removed the guard","It created opposition","It promoted a pawn","It changed the time control"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Removing the defender exposes its target.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":1,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V21-1","V21-2","V21-t"]},{"id":22,"title":"Deflection, decoy, and interference","body":["Deflection draws a defensive piece away from a square or duty. Decoy lures a piece onto a vulnerable square, where a tactic can follow.","Interference places a piece between a defender and its target, interrupting a line of protection.","The common theme is changing *which squares the pieces can actually defend*. Always verify the opponent’s alternative responses."],"takeaways":["Deflect a defender","Decoy a target","Interrupt a line"],"target":"Distinguish deflection, decoy and interference on the board.","worked":"","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V22-1","lesson":22,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A sacrifice pulls a queen away from defending its king. Which motif is most relevant?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Deflection","Opposition","Underpromotion","Zugzwang"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The queen is redirected from a critical defensive duty.","hint":"What was the piece defending before it moved?"},{"id":"V22-2","lesson":22,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Placing a bishop between a rook and the rook’s defended pawn is an example of…","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A pawn race","Interference","Castling","A perpetual check"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Blocking the defender’s line is an interference tactic.","hint":"Picture the line from defender to target."},{"id":"V22-t","lesson":22,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A bishop can block a rook’s line to its defended queen. What should you evaluate?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Whether interference creates a forcing follow-up","Whether the bishop can jump pieces","Whether the king has castled twice","Whether the game uses increments"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Line interruption is only useful if the follow-up works.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":1,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V22-1","V22-2","V22-t"]},{"id":23,"title":"Intermediate moves and counter-tactics","body":["A zwischenzug (intermediate move) interrupts an expected capture or recapture with a forcing move—often check.","Before automatically recapturing a piece, check whether you have a stronger forcing move first. In tactical sequences, move order can change everything.","Because an intermediate move delays a recapture, calculate whether the opponent can escape or counterattack afterward."],"takeaways":["Notice automatic recapture urge","Search for checks","Verify after forcing reply"],"target":"Recognize when a forcing move should precede a routine recapture.","worked":"","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V23-1","lesson":23,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the defining feature of a zwischenzug?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A forcing move inserted before an expected continuation","A pawn moving two squares","Any move before castling","The final move of an opening"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It changes the order of events with an immediate threat, often a check.","hint":"It interrupts the obvious sequence."},{"id":"V23-2","lesson":23,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After an opponent captures your bishop, what is worth checking before recapturing?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Whether you have a forcing check first","Whether you can offer a draw","Whether every pawn is defended","Whether you have moved the king before"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An intermediate check may improve your tactical result.","hint":"Look for moves the opponent must answer."},{"id":"V23-t","lesson":23,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You can recapture a pawn or check the enemy king first. What should you calculate?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The intermediate check and its response","Only the automatic recapture","Only pawn structures","Only your next opening"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Move order may alter the tactical sequence.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":1,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V23-1","V23-2","V23-t"]},{"id":24,"title":"Building a mating net","body":["A mating net restricts the king’s flight squares before the final check. Attackers cooperate to control escape routes.","Look for weaknesses near the king: back rank, open files, weak dark squares, and insufficient defenders. A lone queen attack often fails.","Count defenders as well as attackers, and never assume an apparent threat is forced without calculating the defense."],"takeaways":["Count flight squares","Coordinate pieces","Verify defense"],"target":"Explain how several pieces cooperate to trap the king.","worked":"","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V24-1","lesson":24,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the main goal when surrounding the enemy king with several attacking pieces?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Take away all safe squares so the king cannot escape check.","Capture every pawn before giving check.","Promote a bishop into a queen.","Give exactly three checks in a row."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A 'mating net' is an arrangement of pieces that restricts the king's escape squares and can lead to checkmate.","hint":"Think about the squares to which the king could escape."},{"id":"V24-2","lesson":24,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why can a lone queen attack be dangerous for the attacker?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The queen has no legal moves","It may be chased while defenders develop","Queens cannot check kings alone","It forces stalemate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Premature solo queen attacks can lose time and allow the opponent to mobilize.","hint":"Consider coordination and tempo."},{"id":"V24-t","lesson":24,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your queen checks, but the opponent king can escape easily. What did you overlook?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Flight squares","Opening name","Move number","Material values"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A mating net requires restricting the king’s safe exits.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":1,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V24-1","V24-2","V24-t"]},{"id":25,"title":"Opening development and tempo","body":["Each player takes turns making one move. Chess players sometimes call a useful turn a **tempo** (plural: tempi). For example, developing a knight while attacking the opponent's queen can be especially useful: you improve your knight while the queen must respond.","**Gaining time** means using a move to improve your position and force your opponent to react. It does not mean adding seconds to the clock. **Losing time** (or losing a tempo) means spending a turn without useful progress, such as moving the same piece again for no reason while the opponent develops other pieces.","This is a guideline, not an absolute rule. If your bishop is threatened, moving it again may be necessary. After ten moves, compare king safety, the number of developed pieces and control of the center."],"takeaways":["Tempo = a turn or move used productively.","Gaining time = improve a piece while forcing a response.","Moving a piece twice is fine when there is a good reason."],"target":"Develop efficiently while allowing justified exceptions.","worked":"","refs":["S03","S05"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V25-1","lesson":25,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why is repeatedly moving the same piece in the opening sometimes a problem?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["You may use up moves without improving your position while the opponent develops several pieces.","Moving a piece twice automatically loses a pawn.","Moving a piece twice immediately ends the game.","Moving a piece twice means the king is in check."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each move is a turn you could use to improve a piece or defend a threat. A useful turn is called a tempo; spending extra turns without a good reason is called losing time or losing tempi.","hint":"Compare how many pieces each player has brought into the game."},{"id":"V25-2","lesson":25,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which is a sound reason to move an already-developed knight again?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It avoids losing the knight to a tactical threat","You always must move knights twice","You have no reason to castle","You want to imitate a grandmaster without checking"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Concrete threats sometimes override the rule against repeated moves.","hint":"Rules have tactical exceptions."},{"id":"V25-t","lesson":25,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You moved your bishop twice to avoid capture. Was that necessarily wrong?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["No; a concrete threat can justify it","Yes; moving pieces twice is illegal","Yes; all bishops must stay home","No; tempo never matters"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Threats can justify a second move.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":2,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V25-1","V25-2","V25-t"]},{"id":26,"title":"Pawn centers and opening pawn breaks","body":["Pawns define central space and restrict pieces. A pawn chain has a base, and pressure against that base can destabilize the whole structure.","Pawn breaks, such as d4 in many Italian positions or ...c5 against White’s d4 center, challenge the opponent’s formation.","Before pushing, calculate what happens if the pawn is captured. A well-timed break opens lines for your pieces; a premature break can leave weaknesses."],"takeaways":["Identify chain base","Prepare breaks","Calculate exchanges"],"target":"Recognize when and why to challenge a pawn center.","worked":"","refs":["S03","S05","S13"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V26-1","lesson":26,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why does c3 frequently support d4 in the Italian Game?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It prepares a central pawn advance","It castles immediately","It attacks the king on e8","It forces a queen trade"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The c-pawn can reinforce a later d4 pawn break.","hint":"Consider which pawn c3 supports."},{"id":"V26-2","lesson":26,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Before playing a central pawn break, which calculation matters most?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["What follows if the pawn is captured","Whether the pawn matches a memorized diagram","Whether you have played your h-pawn","Whether the move is aesthetically pleasing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The exchange sequence decides whether the break wins space or loses material.","hint":"Calculate the most forcing reply."},{"id":"V26-t","lesson":26,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your pawn break leaves a hanging bishop. What should you do?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Calculate the exchanges and loose bishop before committing","Push automatically","Ignore all threats","Trade kings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tactical safety controls pawn-break timing.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":2,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V26-1","V26-2","V26-t"]},{"id":27,"title":"White opening choices: Scotch and Ruy Lopez","body":["The Scotch Game begins 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.d4, challenging the center immediately. White often accepts open lines and early exchanges.","The Ruy Lopez begins 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5. White indirectly increases pressure on e5 by engaging its c6 defender. It often leads to slower strategic play.","Compare their pawn structures and middlegame plans before choosing. Neither opening guarantees an advantage or a win."],"takeaways":["Scotch: immediate d4","Ruy Lopez: bishop pressure","Choose by plans, not traps"],"target":"Compare a direct central break with pressure against a defender.","worked":"","refs":["S03"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["e2e4","Claim central space."],["e7e5","Black occupies e5."],["g1f3","Develop and attack e5."],["b8c6","Defend with development."],["f1b5","Ruy Lopez: pressure the c6 defender."]],"exercises":[{"id":"V27-1","lesson":27,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which move defines the Scotch Game after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["3.d4","3.h4","3.a3","3.g3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"3.d4 challenges the central e5 pawn immediately.","hint":"One choice pushes a central pawn."},{"id":"V27-2","lesson":27,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What does 3.Bb5 primarily pressure in the Ruy Lopez?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The c6 knight supporting e5","The h7 pawn","The rook on h8 directly","The king on e8 with check"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bb5 develops the bishop and pressures the e5 defender.","hint":"Think about what guards Black’s e5 pawn."},{"id":"V27-t","lesson":27,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You enjoy immediate central clashes. Which style might you explore first?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Scotch Game","A setup with only a-pawn pushes","An opening where White never plays a pawn","A forced endgame without pieces"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Scotch contests e5 with an early d4.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":3,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V27-1","V27-2","V27-t"]},{"id":28,"title":"White opening choices: Queen’s Gambit and London","body":["After 1.d4 d5 2.c4, the Queen’s Gambit offers the c-pawn to challenge Black’s central pawn structure. It is a classical fight for central control.","The London System often develops a bishop early with Bf4 and builds a solid d4-based setup. It is flexible but does not eliminate the need to respond to the opponent’s moves.","Compare active central tension with repeatable development plans. Both demand tactical awareness."],"takeaways":["Queen’s Gambit: central tension","London: flexible setup","Do not play on autopilot"],"target":"Describe two different ways to build a White opening repertoire.","worked":"","refs":["S07","S13"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["d2d4","Occupy the center."],["d7d5","Black responds symmetrically."],["c2c4","The Queen’s Gambit challenges d5."]],"exercises":[{"id":"V28-1","lesson":28,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is White’s primary strategic goal with 1.d4 d5 2.c4?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Challenge Black’s d5 center","Force an immediate checkmate","Make castling illegal","Win the h7 pawn directly"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The c-pawn challenges Black’s central foothold on d5.","hint":"Focus on central pawn tension."},{"id":"V28-2","lesson":28,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which bishop development is typical of many London setups?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Bf4","Bh6","Ba6","Bxc8 on move two"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bf4 activates the c1 bishop before the e-pawn may block it.","hint":"Which bishop square keeps the piece outside its pawn chain?"},{"id":"V28-t","lesson":28,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent immediately attacks your London bishop. What should you do?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Respond to the real threat, not just a memorized setup","Play your next planned move regardless","Move the king for no reason","Offer a draw"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Opening systems do not remove the need for calculation.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":3,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V28-1","V28-2","V28-t"]},{"id":29,"title":"Black opening choices: French and Sicilian","body":["The French Defense starts 1.e4 e6, aiming for ...d5 on the next move. It creates characteristic pawn chains and tension around White’s e4 pawn.","The Sicilian Defense starts 1.e4 c5, challenging central control asymmetrically and often creating unbalanced middlegames.","They have very different structures and theory burdens. Start by understanding common pawn breaks and development instead of memorizing sharp variations."],"takeaways":["French: ...e6 then ...d5","Sicilian: ...c5","Recognize different structures"],"target":"Distinguish two principled replies to 1.e4.","worked":"","refs":["S03"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["e2e4","White claims the center."],["c7c5","Sicilian: asymmetrical central challenge."]],"exercises":[{"id":"V29-1","lesson":29,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which first move defines the French Defense against 1.e4?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["...e6","...g6","...a6","...h5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"1...e6 prepares to challenge the center with ...d5.","hint":"It prepares the d-pawn push."},{"id":"V29-2","lesson":29,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What makes 1...c5 (Sicilian) distinct from 1...e5?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It challenges the center asymmetrically","It blocks both bishops forever","It immediately checks White","It prevents White playing d4"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Black contests White’s d4 influence from the c-file rather than copying e5.","hint":"Consider which pawn Black moves."},{"id":"V29-t","lesson":29,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You are choosing a Black reply to e4. What matters besides its name?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Typical pawn structures and middlegame plans","Only internet popularity","Whether it guarantees a win","Whether it avoids all tactics"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plans and positions determine whether an opening suits you.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":3,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V29-1","V29-2","V29-t"]},{"id":30,"title":"Black opening choices: Slav, QGD and King’s Indian","body":["Against 1.d4, the Queen’s Gambit Declined uses ...d5 and ...e6, reinforcing d5 but temporarily restricting the c8 bishop.","The Slav Defense uses ...d5 and ...c6 to support d5 while leaving the light-squared bishop a freer development route. The King’s Indian Defense allows White broad central space, later counterattacking with pieces and pawn breaks.","Choose based on how comfortable you are defending space and handling different pawn structures."],"takeaways":["QGD: ...e6","Slav: ...c6","King’s Indian: counterattack the center"],"target":"Compare central support structures without committing to one repertoire yet.","worked":"","refs":["S07","S13"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[["d2d4","White claims the center."],["d7d5","Black takes a central foothold."],["c2c4","White challenges d5."],["c7c6","The Slav support structure."]],"exercises":[{"id":"V30-1","lesson":30,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the defining central support move in the Slav Defense after 1.d4 d5 2.c4?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["...c6","...h6","...a5","...f5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"...c6 supports the d5 pawn without immediately closing the c8 bishop.","hint":"Which flank pawn reinforces d5?"},{"id":"V30-2","lesson":30,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a common strategic theme in the King’s Indian Defense?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Allow space and later strike back at the center","Force a queen trade on move two","Castle queenside in every game","Exchange all knights immediately"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Black often permits a broad White center and challenges it later.","hint":"Think counterplay against central space."},{"id":"V30-t","lesson":30,"kind":"mc","prompt":"An opening often concedes White central space. How should Black think?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Prepare a timely counterattack against the center","Assume the game is lost","Sacrifice everything","Avoid development forever"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A flexible counterattack can challenge a broad pawn center.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":3,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V30-1","V30-2","V30-t"]},{"id":31,"title":"Open files and active rooks","body":["Rooks gain power on files with few or no pawns. On an open file, they can penetrate a position and attack the seventh rank.","Doubling rooks may increase pressure, but only when the file or target justifies the time spent. A rook behind a passed pawn is often more useful than a rook defending an irrelevant pawn elsewhere.","Ask: which file could become open after the next pawn exchange?"],"takeaways":["Identify open files","Target entry squares","Coordinate rooks"],"target":"Improve rook activity through files and targets.","worked":"","refs":["S01"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V31-1","lesson":31,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why can a rook often move more freely along a file (vertical column) with no pawns on it?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["No pawns block the rook's movement up and down the file.","The rook becomes worth nine points.","The rook may jump over other pieces.","No enemy piece can attack it there."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An open file is a vertical column with no pawns. Rooks can travel and attack along it without pawns obstructing their path.","hint":"Consider which pieces can block a rook on a column."},{"id":"V31-2","lesson":31,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a useful question before doubling rooks?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["What target or entry square will the pressure create?","How many moves will the queen make next?","Will my bishops become knights?","Will it end the game immediately?"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Doubling is worthwhile when it serves a plan, not merely because it looks coordinated.","hint":"Look for a purpose."},{"id":"V31-t","lesson":31,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your rook occupies an open file but has no entry square. What next?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Find a concrete target or plan","Double every rook automatically","Trade your king","Ignore opponent threats"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Activity is useful when it creates actual pressure.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":4,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V31-1","V31-2","V31-t"]},{"id":32,"title":"Pawn structure: isolated and backward pawns","body":["An isolated pawn has no friendly pawn on either adjacent file. It can be a weakness because no pawn can defend it, but it can also grant space and open lines for pieces.","A backward pawn lags behind its neighbors and may struggle to advance safely. The square in front often becomes a useful blockade point.","Evaluate both the static weaknesses and dynamic activity: a pawn is not automatically bad just because it is isolated."],"takeaways":["Locate isolated pawns","Understand blockade squares","Balance activity against weakness"],"target":"Describe the tradeoff between static pawn weakness and active pieces.","worked":"","refs":["S01"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V32-1","lesson":32,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which pawn is isolated?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A pawn with no friendly pawn on adjacent files","A pawn defended by two pawns","A pawn that has reached rank eight","A pawn directly next to its own king"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Without adjacent friendly pawns, that pawn lacks pawn support.","hint":"Look at the files on each side."},{"id":"V32-2","lesson":32,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why might a side accept an isolated queen’s pawn?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It may gain active piece play and open lines","It makes its king immune to check","It prevents all trades","It guarantees promotion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Activity and initiative can compensate for a long-term pawn weakness.","hint":"Look beyond the static pawn shape."},{"id":"V32-t","lesson":32,"kind":"mc","prompt":"An isolated pawn gives you open files and active pieces. How should you judge it?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Balance activity against the weakness","Assume any isolated pawn loses","Assume it always wins","Ignore pawn structure"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Dynamic compensation can offset pawn-structure weaknesses.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":4,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V32-1","V32-2","V32-t"]},{"id":33,"title":"Outposts and improving minor pieces","body":["An outpost is a square that an enemy pawn cannot easily challenge. Knights often thrive there, especially near the center.","A bishop may be “bad” when its own pawn chain limits its movement, but it can still serve a defensive function. Look for pawn breaks and useful diagonals before trading it.","Improvement means giving pieces better tasks, not just moving them onto more central squares mechanically."],"takeaways":["Seek outposts","Evaluate pawn color complexes","Improve worst piece"],"target":"Identify durable knight squares and purposeful bishop improvement.","worked":"","refs":["S01"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V33-1","lesson":33,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which is an important quality of a knight outpost?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Enemy pawns cannot easily chase it away","The knight is on the rim","The knight attacks its own king","It must be protected by a rook"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A durable outpost is difficult for enemy pawns to challenge.","hint":"Consider the enemy pawn structure."},{"id":"V33-2","lesson":33,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A bishop is blocked by many pawns of its own square color. What may help?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A well-timed pawn break","Moving the king immediately into the center","Trading both rooks at any cost","Ignoring its movement for the whole game"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing the pawn structure can open a useful diagonal for the bishop.","hint":"Think about opening lines."},{"id":"V33-t","lesson":33,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A knight looks centralized but an enemy pawn can immediately chase it away. Is it a durable outpost?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["No; a pawn can challenge it","Yes; all center squares are safe","Yes; knights cannot be chased","Only if both queens remain"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Durable outposts resist pawn challenges.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":4,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V33-1","V33-2","V33-t"]},{"id":34,"title":"Attack planning and when not to sacrifice","body":["A successful attack usually depends on development, access to the enemy king, piece coordination, and concrete tactics.","A sacrifice may be sound if it forces mate, wins enough material, or secures lasting compensation; it is not automatically correct because it “looks attacking.”","Before sacrificing, calculate the best defensive response and check whether your attack runs out of threats."],"takeaways":["Count attacking pieces","Find defenders","Calculate sacrifice outcome"],"target":"Evaluate attacking chances with calculation, not excitement alone.","worked":"","refs":["S01","S09"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V34-1","lesson":34,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which is the strongest justification for sacrificing a bishop?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A calculated forced mate","It seems stylish","The bishop has moved twice","It happens in a famous opening trap"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A forced mate provides concrete justification; other sacrifices require clear compensation.","hint":"Look for forcing outcomes."},{"id":"V34-2","lesson":34,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What should you calculate before launching a king attack?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The opponent’s best defensive replies","Only the move you hope they play","Only your next pawn push","The number of videos on the opening"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sound attacks must survive the opponent’s strongest defense.","hint":"Assume your opponent finds a good response."},{"id":"V34-t","lesson":34,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You can sacrifice a rook for an attack but see a strong defense. What next?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Calculate that defense before sacrificing","Ignore it and sacrifice","Resign","Assume all sacrifices work"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sound sacrifices require concrete consequences.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":4,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V34-1","V34-2","V34-t"]},{"id":35,"title":"King activity and opposition","body":["In many simplified endgames, the king becomes an active fighting piece. It can support passed pawns, restrict the opposing king, and capture weak pawns.","**Opposition** is when two kings face each other on the same rank or file with exactly one empty square between them. The player whose king must move first may need to give way. The player who gets the useful king position can benefit, but opposition is not an automatic win.","A pawn race is a contest to promote pawns. Count the actual moves (also called tempi), see whether either king can stop a pawn, and don't assume an active king will win by itself."],"takeaways":["Activate king","Use opposition","Count pawn moves"],"target":"Use the king actively while respecting opposition and tactics.","worked":"","refs":["S01"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V35-1","lesson":35,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why does king activity often increase in pawn endgames?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["With fewer attacking pieces, the king can safely help pawns","The king becomes a queen","Kings may move two squares","Checks are no longer legal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With major threats reduced, the king supports and attacks pawns directly.","hint":"Compare the number of remaining attackers."},{"id":"V35-2","lesson":35,"kind":"mc","prompt":"If two kings face each other with one empty square between them, and one king must move away first, does this alone guarantee a win?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["No. Pawn positions, turn order and possible pawn moves still matter.","Yes. Whichever king moves second always wins.","Only if a queen is on the board.","Only when both kings are on the a-file."],"answer":0,"explanation":"That king relationship is called opposition. It can be useful for taking space, but you still have to calculate whether a pawn can advance or promote.","hint":"Look at the pawns and other legal moves, not just the kings."},{"id":"V35-t","lesson":35,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Both players are racing to promote pawns, but your king is active. What should you calculate first?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["How many moves each pawn needs to promote, and whether a king can stop it.","Only how close your king is to the center.","The name of the opening used earlier.","Only how many moves have passed since move ten."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Count the moves (sometimes called tempi) each pawn needs and check whether a king can stop the promotion. An active king alone does not guarantee success.","hint":"Compare each pawn's distance from its promotion square."}],"module":5,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V35-1","V35-2","V35-t"]},{"id":36,"title":"The square of the pawn and passed pawn races","body":["The square rule quickly estimates whether an enemy king can catch an unopposed passed pawn. Imagine a square extending from the pawn to its promotion square; king position, whose move it is and double pawn moves matter.","A pawn race can also involve checks, captures and support from friendly kings. The simple square rule is a starting point, not a replacement for calculating legal moves.","Practice counting both sides’ tempi instead of assuming the fastest-looking pawn wins."],"takeaways":["Apply square rule","Count tempos","Watch for checks"],"target":"Decide when a passed pawn can be stopped and check tactical exceptions.","worked":"","refs":["S01"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V36-1","lesson":36,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A pawn is trying to reach the last row and promote. What does the 'square rule' help you work out?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Whether the defending king can catch the pawn before it promotes.","Whether an opposing rook is pinned.","Which opening was played in the game.","How many moves a knight could make."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The square rule is a quick way to estimate whether a king can catch a passed pawn. You can also count the king's and pawn's moves directly.","hint":"Compare the king's distance with the pawn's remaining moves."},{"id":"V36-2","lesson":36,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why may a simple pawn-race count be wrong?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Checks and captures can change the sequence","Pawns cannot promote","The 50-move rule always ends first","Kings may pass their turn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Forcing moves and support can alter the apparent race.","hint":"Count forcing interruptions."},{"id":"V36-t","lesson":36,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent king is outside the pawn square, but has a checking capture available. What next?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Calculate the tactical exception","Declare promotion certain","Ignore capture","Pass your turn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Forcing moves can invalidate a simple square-rule impression.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":5,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V36-1","V36-2","V36-t"]},{"id":37,"title":"Rook endgames: activity and passed pawns","body":["Rook endgames are common and often drawish even with an extra pawn. Activity matters greatly: an active rook can attack pawns from behind or check the king from afar.","In many positions, the rook belongs behind the passed pawn—your own or the opponent’s—because its line of influence grows as the pawn advances. This guideline has exceptions.","Before trading into a rook ending, consider king activity, rook activity, and the location of passed pawns."],"takeaways":["Activate rook","Rook behind passer","Examine checks"],"target":"Apply basic rook endgame activity rules without treating them as absolutes.","worked":"","refs":["S01"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V37-1","lesson":37,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When a pawn has a clear path toward promotion, where is a friendly rook often most useful?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Behind the advancing pawn, where the rook can support it.","Directly in front of the pawn, blocking its path.","Always beside your own king, even if far away.","On any other column without a clear purpose."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A passed pawn has no enemy pawns able to stop its advance on its own or adjacent files. A rook behind it often supports the pawn as it advances.","hint":"Which rook position keeps the pawn's forward path clear?"},{"id":"V37-2","lesson":37,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why is an extra pawn not always enough to win a rook endgame?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Active defense and checking can hold a draw","Rooks cannot give check","Pawn promotion is illegal with rooks","The side with more pawns always wins"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rook activity and king position can outweigh a small material edge.","hint":"Think about defensive checking."},{"id":"V37-t","lesson":37,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You have an extra pawn in a rook endgame but the opponent has active checks. What matters?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["King and rook activity alongside material","Material alone","Opening theory only","How fast you click"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Active defense can hold an extra-pawn ending.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":5,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V37-1","V37-2","V37-t"]},{"id":38,"title":"Converting advantages without stalemate","body":["When ahead in material, reduce the defender’s counterplay. Trade pieces when the resulting ending is clearly winning, but do not exchange into a drawn pawn ending automatically.","For basic mates, use the king with the queen or rook to restrict the opponent’s king. Be alert to stalemate: a side without legal moves is drawn if it is not in check.","Take your time, use forcing moves carefully, and verify the defender has a legal reply when it is not checkmate."],"takeaways":["Reduce counterplay","Support heavy pieces with king","Avoid stalemate"],"target":"Simplify winning positions and avoid accidental draws.","worked":"","refs":["S11"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V38-1","lesson":38,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which position is stalemate?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A side to move has no legal moves and is not in check","A king is attacked and has no legal moves","A side has only its king left","Both sides have queens"],"answer":0,"explanation":"No legal move without check is stalemate; with check, it is checkmate.","hint":"Differentiate check from legal moves."},{"id":"V38-2","lesson":38,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why might trading pieces be useful when clearly ahead?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It can reduce counterplay and simplify conversion","It automatically creates mate","It always wins a pawn","It makes all remaining moves legal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Simplification can make it easier to turn a real material advantage into a win.","hint":"Think about your opponent’s remaining threats."},{"id":"V38-t","lesson":38,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You are up a queen but the opponent king has no legal move and is not in check. What is the result?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Draw by stalemate","Win by resignation","Forced checkmate","King promotion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A player without legal moves but not in check is stalemated.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":5,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V38-1","V38-2","V38-t"]},{"id":39,"title":"Annotating your games before the engine","body":["Your own reasoning is vital. Before opening Stockfish, identify the first move where you were surprised and write down what you thought was happening.","Then compare your idea with the engine’s top continuation. Distinguish a tactical blunder from a strategic alternative rather than assuming every non-best move was a mistake.","End each review with one actionable habit to use in the next game."],"takeaways":["Annotate first","Check engine second","Write one next-game habit"],"target":"Turn reviewed games into specific practice goals.","worked":"","refs":["S03","S10"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V39-1","lesson":39,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why should you annotate your thinking before engine analysis?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It preserves your actual decision process","It makes Stockfish stronger","It changes the legal moves","It guarantees a higher rating next game"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Your notes show the misconception to correct, rather than only the engine’s preferred move.","hint":"Remember your thought process."},{"id":"V39-2","lesson":39,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What makes a useful takeaway from a review?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["One repeatable decision habit","The exact engine score only","A promise to never lose again","Memorizing every move in the game"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A concrete habit transfers to future decisions more reliably than a raw evaluation.","hint":"What could you do differently next game?"},{"id":"V39-t","lesson":39,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Stockfish says your move was inferior. What should you write in your notes?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The threat or idea you missed","Only an exclamation mark","That engines never err","That a rating point was stolen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Record the transferable mistake.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":6,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V39-1","V39-2","V39-t"]},{"id":40,"title":"Calculation checks and structured self-review","body":["Calculate forcing moves in short branches: candidate, opponent’s best reply, your continuation, then evaluate the final position.","Do not choose a move because the first two plies look good. Check for in-between moves, king safety, and material balance at the end of the line.","After a missed exercise, explain the error to yourself in a phrase (“I missed the defender,” “I ignored check”). The course will schedule related review later."],"takeaways":["Candidate and reply","Re-evaluate endpoint","Review the missed motif"],"target":"Apply a consistent calculation process and learn from failed lines.","worked":"","refs":["S01","S10"],"fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","line":[],"exercises":[{"id":"V40-1","lesson":40,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When is it most useful to evaluate a calculated line?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["After considering the opponent’s strongest response","Immediately after your first move only","After copying engine notation with no thought","Only after the game ends"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The result depends on the full relevant sequence, not just your first idea.","hint":"Imagine the opponent defending well."},{"id":"V40-2","lesson":40,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a helpful note after missing a tactical puzzle?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The specific pattern you overlooked","The number of pieces on the starting board","Your favorite opening name","That every engine score is perfect"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Naming the missed motif helps you recognize it in a new position.","hint":"Identify the transferable cause."},{"id":"V40-t","lesson":40,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You solved a puzzle only after two hints. Should that count as fully mastered?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Not yet; repeat it without hints later","Yes; any solved attempt is mastery","No; you must restart chess entirely","Only if the puzzle is a fork"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Independent recall is a better indicator of mastery.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}],"module":6,"length":25,"sourceNote":"Original instruction informed by the listed chess teaching references.","exerciseIds":["V40-1","V40-2","V40-t"]}],"sources":[{"id":"S01","title":"The Steps Method — Manual Step 2","about":"The published topic sequence: piece activity, double attacks, pins, discovered attacks, intermediate moves, opening rules, pawn endings.","url":"https://www.chess-steps.com/978-90-77275-36-8-manual-step-2"},{"id":"S02","title":"The Steps Method — Official overview","about":"Six-step trainer-and-workbook structure with progressively harder exercises.","url":"https://www.stappenmethode.nl/en/"},{"id":"S03","title":"Chess.com — Study Plan: The Opening (Sam Shankland)","about":"Opening principles, practical play, checking positions after move 10, and a few studied tournament openings.","url":"https://www.chess.com/article/view/study-plan-for-beginners-the-opening"},{"id":"S04","title":"Chess.com — How Lessons Work","about":"Explanations followed by interactive challenges organized by level.","url":"https://support.chess.com/en/articles/8609703-how-do-lessons-work-on-chess-com"},{"id":"S05","title":"Chess.com Opening Encyclopedia — Italian Game","about":"Definition, Giuoco Piano, Pianissimo, Two Knights, and main opening ideas.","url":"https://www.chess.com/openings/Italian-Game"},{"id":"S06","title":"Chess.com Opening Encyclopedia — Caro-Kann Defense","about":"Defining moves and Classical, Advance, and Exchange variations; bishop-development ideas.","url":"https://www.chess.com/openings/Caro-Kann-Defense"},{"id":"S07","title":"Chess.com Opening Encyclopedia — Queen’s Gambit Declined","about":"1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6 definition and basic plans.","url":"https://www.chess.com/openings/Queens-Gambit-Declined"},{"id":"S08","title":"Chess.com Opening Encyclopedia — QGD Orthodox Defense","about":"Published Orthodox move order and solid kingside development plan.","url":"https://www.chess.com/openings/Queens-Gambit-Declined-Orthodox-Defense"},{"id":"S09","title":"Lichess — Puzzle Themes","about":"Definitions and targeted practice categories for forks, pins, skewers, checks, tactics, and opening puzzles.","url":"https://lichess.org/training/themes"},{"id":"S10","title":"Chess.com — Spaced Repetition Scheduling","about":"Review intervals increasing with successful recall; mistakes shorten the intervals.","url":"https://support.chess.com/en/articles/10319322-how-does-the-spaced-repetition-scheduling-work"},{"id":"S11","title":"Susan Polgar Foundation — Free Chess Curriculum","about":"A published beginner curriculum combining notation, opening principles, tactics and checkmating skills.","url":"https://susanpolgarfoundation.org/free-curriculum/"},{"id":"S12","title":"Chess.com Opening Encyclopedia — Caro-Kann Classical","about":"Verified Classical move order and the ...Bf5 idea.","url":"https://www.chess.com/openings/Caro-Kann-Defense-Classical-Variation"},{"id":"S13","title":"Chess.com — The Queen’s Gambit overview","about":"Explanation of ...c5 as a common Black counterattack to White’s central pawn chain.","url":"https://www.chess.com/openings/Queens-Gambit"}],"exercises":[{"id":"V01-a1","lesson":1,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After your opponent moves, what should you identify before choosing your response?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Their immediate threats","Your favorite opening name","The move you planned five turns ago","Whether the clock is green"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Scan the changed threats first.","hint":"Think about opponent intent."},{"id":"V01-a2","lesson":1,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which search sequence often finds tactics efficiently?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Checks, captures and threats","Only quiet pawn moves","Only queen moves","Only king moves"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Forcing moves constrain the response tree.","hint":"Consider moves needing immediate answers."},{"id":"P01","lesson":2,"kind":"board","prompt":"Find the knight fork that checks Black and attacks the rook on a8.","fen":"r3k3/8/8/1N6/8/8/8/4K3 w - - 0 1","answer":"b5c7","explanation":"A knight from b5 jumps to c7 with check, also attacking a8.","hint":"Look for a knight check that attacks a second target."},{"id":"P16","lesson":2,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After the fork Nc7+, which valuable Black piece is attacked alongside the king?","fen":"r3k3/8/8/1N6/8/8/8/4K3 w - - 0 1","answer":0,"explanation":"The knight on c7 attacks the rook on a8 as well as the king on e8.","hint":"Trace the knight’s possible jumps from c7.","options":["The rook on a8.","The queen on d8.","The bishop on c8.","The rook on h8."]},{"id":"V02-a1","lesson":2,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why may a knight fork fail despite attacking two pieces?","fen":"r3k3/8/8/1N6/8/8/8/4K3 w - - 0 1","options":["The knight may be captured or an enemy check may intervene","Knights cannot fork rooks","Forked pieces always escape","Forks count as illegal moves"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Calculate whether the forking piece survives.","hint":"Inspect the reply."},{"id":"P02","lesson":3,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why is White’s knight on f3 relatively pinned?","fen":"4k3/8/8/8/6b1/5N2/8/3QK3 w - - 0 1","answer":1,"explanation":"The bishop on g4 attacks the queen on d1 through the knight. Moving it is legal but may lose the queen.","hint":"Trace the diagonal behind the knight.","options":["It is illegal for White to move the knight, because the king would be checked.","The bishop points through the knight toward White’s queen.","The knight is pinned by Black’s queen to White’s rook.","The knight can move, but doing so always loses the king."]},{"id":"P03","lesson":3,"kind":"board","prompt":"Find the rook check that skewers the Black king and queen.","fen":"q7/8/k7/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","answer":"e1a1","explanation":"Ra1+ forces Black to resolve check, leaving the queen on a8 exposed.","hint":"A rook wants to line up with the king and queen."},{"id":"P04","lesson":3,"kind":"board","prompt":"Find the bishop move that gives double check.","fen":"4k3/8/8/8/4B3/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","answer":"e4g6","explanation":"Bg6+ opens the rook’s e-file attack AND attacks the king from g6.","hint":"Moving the bishop also uncovers a rook attack."},{"id":"V03-a1","lesson":3,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What distinguishes an absolute pin from a relative pin?","fen":"4k3/8/8/8/6b1/5N2/8/3QK3 w - - 0 1","options":["An absolute pin exposes the king","A relative pin is always illegal","An absolute pin needs a queen","Pins only involve knights"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The king may not be left in check, so an absolute pin restricts legal moves.","hint":"Which piece is behind the pinned one?"},{"id":"P05","lesson":4,"kind":"board","prompt":"White to move: find checkmate in one.","fen":"4r1k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","answer":"e1e8","explanation":"Rxe8# is mate: the king has no flight square, block, or useful capture.","hint":"Look at the e-file and the trapped back rank."},{"id":"V04-a1","lesson":4,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When can making a quiet developing move be dangerous?","fen":"4r1k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","options":["When an immediate forcing threat goes unanswered","When you control the center","When you have castled","When queens are on the board"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Meeting tactical threats takes priority over general improvement.","hint":"Threats come before plans."},{"id":"V04-a2","lesson":4,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which question belongs in a safe-move checklist?","fen":"4r1k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","options":["What is my opponent’s strongest reply?","Can I move a rook early regardless of the position?","Can I ignore checks to build space?","Can I stop my opponent from thinking?"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Calculate a credible reply to your candidate move.","hint":"Picture the opponent’s turn."},{"id":"P06","lesson":5,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What are the two main jobs of 3.Bc4 in the Italian Game?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","e7e5","g1f3","b8c6","f1c4"]},"answer":1,"explanation":"The bishop develops actively and bears down on f7, a sensitive square early in the game.","hint":"Consider both piece activity and the attacked diagonal.","options":["It defends the queen and attacks the a8 rook.","It develops the bishop and creates pressure against f7.","It immediately forces checkmate on f7.","It prevents Black from castling on either side."]},{"id":"V05-a1","lesson":5,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why should knights and bishops usually develop early?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["They become useful on active squares","They immediately promote","They make pawn moves illegal","They prevent all checks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Developed pieces influence key squares.","hint":"Think piece mobility."},{"id":"V05-a2","lesson":5,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the point of castling in many openings?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Improve king safety and connect a rook toward play","Win a pawn automatically","Check the opponent","Force a draw"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Castling relocates king and rook with one move.","hint":"What happens to two pieces?"},{"id":"P23","lesson":6,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent plays an unfamiliar move on move four. What should you do first?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","answer":0,"explanation":"Inspect the threat, examine forcing moves, then choose a sound developing or central response.","hint":"Avoid assuming the opponent must follow your prepared line.","options":["Find their threat, look for checks and captures, then choose a safe useful move.","Continue memorized moves regardless of threats.","Trade queens immediately to avoid calculations.","Always move the same developed piece again."]},{"id":"V06-a1","lesson":6,"kind":"mc","prompt":"If your opponent departs from a memorized line, what matters most?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Respond to the actual threats and position","Play the next memorized move automatically","Resign immediately","Trade your queen as soon as possible"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Opening principles are guides, not forced scripts.","hint":"Follow the current board."},{"id":"V06-a2","lesson":6,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What should you avoid when learning openings?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Memorizing without understanding plans","Studying central pawn breaks","Studying typical pawn structures","Checking king safety"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plans transfer to unfamiliar move orders.","hint":"Look for transferable knowledge."},{"id":"P07","lesson":7,"kind":"board","prompt":"In this Italian Game position, which White pawn move prepares the central d4 advance?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","e7e5","g1f3","b8c6","f1c4","f8c5"]},"answer":"c2c3","explanation":"c3 supports d4 and leaves White a useful central expansion plan.","hint":"Which pawn can help the d-pawn advance safely?"},{"id":"V07-a1","lesson":7,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why does Bc4 in the Italian Game aim at f7?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It targets a pawn initially defended only by Black’s king","It pins Black’s queen to the rook","It gives check immediately","It traps White’s own knight"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The f7 square is sensitive before Black completes development.","hint":"Look at the bishop diagonal."},{"id":"V07-a2","lesson":7,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What does White often prepare with c3?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The central d4 pawn break","A kingside pawn promotion","Castling queenside immediately","A perpetual check"],"answer":0,"explanation":"c3 can support d4.","hint":"Think of the pawn on d2."},{"id":"P08","lesson":8,"kind":"board","prompt":"White to move: find the central pawn break that challenges e5 and the bishop on c5.","fen":{"after":["e2e4","e7e5","g1f3","b8c6","f1c4","f8c5","c2c3","g8f6"]},"answer":"d2d4","explanation":"d4 attacks the bishop on c5 and challenges Black’s e5 center. ...exd4 is a main reply.","hint":"The c-pawn already supports an advance of the d-pawn."},{"id":"V08-a1","lesson":8,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What should you calculate after playing d4 in an Italian structure?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The exchanges after ...exd4","Whether White’s rook is on a8","Whether c1 is attacked by a knight","Whether the game has ended"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Central breaks often force pawn exchanges.","hint":"Imagine your pawn being captured."},{"id":"V08-a2","lesson":8,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why may an early d4 increase tactical complexity?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It opens central lines","It makes castling illegal","It prevents checks","It promotes the pawn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Open lines help active pieces and create tactical pressure.","hint":"Think about opening diagonals."},{"id":"P09","lesson":9,"kind":"board","prompt":"Black just played ...Nf6 in the Two Knights Defense. What quiet move can White use to protect e4?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","e7e5","g1f3","b8c6","f1c4","g8f6"]},"answer":"d2d3","explanation":"d3 supports e4 and keeps the position quieter than 4.Ng5.","hint":"A central pawn can reinforce e4."},{"id":"P20","lesson":9,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4, which Black replies lead to Giuoco Piano and Two Knights respectively?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","e7e5","g1f3","b8c6","f1c4"]},"answer":0,"explanation":"3...Bc5 is Giuoco Piano; 3...Nf6 is Two Knights Defense, attacking e4.","hint":"One response develops the bishop, the other the knight.","options":["3...Bc5 = Giuoco Piano; 3...Nf6 = Two Knights.","3...Bc5 = Two Knights; 3...Nf6 = Giuoco Piano.","3...d6 = Giuoco Piano; 3...Bc5 = Two Knights.","3...Nf6 = Giuoco Piano; 3...a6 = Two Knights."]},{"id":"V09-a1","lesson":9,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the defining move of the Two Knights Defense after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["3...Nf6","3...h5","3...a5","3...g5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Black develops the g8 knight and attacks e4.","hint":"Which knight can challenge e4?"},{"id":"P10","lesson":10,"kind":"board","prompt":"Black to move: choose a useful development square for the light-squared bishop in the Classical Caro-Kann.","fen":{"after":["e2e4","c7c6","d2d4","d7d5","b1c3","d5e4","c3e4"]},"answer":"c8f5","explanation":"...Bf5 develops the bishop actively before ...e6, attacking the knight on e4.","hint":"Develop the c8 bishop outside the pawn chain."},{"id":"V10-a1","lesson":10,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why does the Caro-Kann begin with ...c6?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["To support ...d5","To check the king immediately","To prevent White from castling","To win e4 without moving again"],"answer":0,"explanation":"...c6 underpins the central ...d5 thrust.","hint":"Look at which pawn c6 can defend."},{"id":"V10-a2","lesson":10,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why might Black play ...Bf5 before ...e6?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["To develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain","To checkmate immediately","To castle on the queenside automatically","To pin the a1 rook"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The bishop can develop before ...e6 closes its diagonal.","hint":"Look at c8 bishop freedom."},{"id":"P11","lesson":11,"kind":"board","prompt":"Black to move: where can the bishop develop effectively in the Caro-Kann Advance?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","c7c6","d2d4","d7d5","e4e5"]},"answer":"c8f5","explanation":"...Bf5 is a standard developing choice before ...e6 closes the light bishop’s path.","hint":"The light bishop wants an active square before ...e6."},{"id":"P12","lesson":11,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Exchange Caro-Kann, 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5, which Black pawn recaptured on d5?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","c7c6","d2d4","d7d5","e4d5","c6d5"]},"answer":2,"explanation":"The pawn from c6 captured diagonally on d5, keeping a central pawn and opening the c-file.","hint":"Follow the diagonal capture ...cxd5.","options":["The pawn on e7.","The pawn on d7.","The pawn on c6.","The pawn on f7."]},{"id":"P21","lesson":11,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Caro-Kann Advance 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.e5, what is White’s pawn-chain base and a common Black break?","fen":{"after":["e2e4","c7c6","d2d4","d7d5","e4e5"]},"answer":0,"explanation":"White’s chain base is d4; Black often challenges it with ...c5.","hint":"A chain’s base is the pawn nearer its own side.","options":["d4, and ...c5.","e5, and ...f6.","d4, and ...h5.","e4, and ...b5."]},{"id":"V11-a1","lesson":11,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Advance Caro-Kann, what is a common pawn break for Black?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["...c5 against d4","...h5 against h2","...a5 against a2","...g5 on the first move"],"answer":0,"explanation":"...c5 challenges the base of White’s central pawn chain.","hint":"Which pawn supports e5?"},{"id":"P13","lesson":12,"kind":"board","prompt":"Black to move: find a move that reinforces the d5 pawn in the Queen’s Gambit.","fen":{"after":["d2d4","d7d5","c2c4"]},"answer":"e7e6","explanation":"...e6 defends d5; the position is the Queen’s Gambit Declined.","hint":"The e-pawn can reinforce d5."},{"id":"P14","lesson":12,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In many Queen’s Gambit Declined setups, what is a drawback of an early ...e6?","fen":{"after":["d2d4","d7d5","c2c4","e7e6","b1c3","g8f6","c1g5","f8e7","e2e3","e8g8","g1f3","b8d7"]},"answer":3,"explanation":"...e6 can restrict the bishop on c8. Black often solves the problem through careful development or pawn breaks.","hint":"Consider the diagonal from c8 toward h3.","options":["It blocks the queen’s usual path to d8.","It removes Black’s right to castle.","It places the knight on b8 in check.","It restricts the bishop on c8."]},{"id":"P22","lesson":12,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Queen’s Gambit Declined, what pawn break often challenges White’s d4 center?","fen":{"after":["d2d4","d7d5","c2c4","e7e6"]},"answer":1,"explanation":"...c5 is a common break that contests the d4 pawn and the center. The exact timing matters.","hint":"Look at the c-pawn’s role in challenging d4.","options":["...h6 to attack the king immediately.","...c5 to challenge White’s central pawns.","...a6 to win the queen on d1.","...g5 to prevent White from castling."]},{"id":"V12-a1","lesson":12,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the definition of the QGD after 1.d4 d5 2.c4?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["2...e6","2...h6","2...g5","2...b5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"...e6 reinforces the central pawn on d5.","hint":"Which pawn can defend d5?"},{"id":"V13-a1","lesson":13,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a sensible starting point for a middlegame plan?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Identify your worst-placed piece and key weaknesses","Decide to sacrifice regardless of calculation","Move a random pawn","Copy an unrelated game"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plans respond to the actual position and piece activity.","hint":"Which piece needs help?"},{"id":"V13-a2","lesson":13,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why should a plan be reevaluated?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The opponent changes the position each move","You should never change a plan","It must last ten moves","Only engine moves can change plans"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The opponent’s threats and structural changes affect your priorities.","hint":"Consider new threats."},{"id":"P15","lesson":14,"kind":"board","prompt":"White to move: find a rook move that checks Black's king on a6. After the king escapes, the rook can capture Black's queen on a8.","fen":"q7/8/k7/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","answer":"e1a1","explanation":"Ra1+ checks the king along the a-file. Black must respond to check, and White can then take the queen on a8. A move that creates an urgent threat while improving your position is sometimes called 'gaining time' (or gaining a tempo).","hint":"Move your rook to a square where it attacks the king vertically. Look at the queen behind the king."},{"id":"P17","lesson":14,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why can Black not block only the rook’s e-file check after Bg6+?","fen":"4k3/8/8/8/4B3/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","answer":1,"explanation":"It is double check: both bishop and rook attack the king. Black must move the king.","hint":"Two different lines are delivering check.","options":["Because a bishop can always move twice on a turn.","Because both the bishop and rook are checking, so Black must move the king.","Because the queen cannot block rook checks under chess rules.","Because the bishop pins Black’s queen to the rook."]},{"id":"V14-a1","lesson":14,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When calculating, whose strongest response do you need?","fen":"q7/8/k7/8/8/8/8/4R1K1 w - - 0 1","options":["Your opponent’s","Only your own","Nobody’s until game review","Only the opening book’s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A line works only if it survives the best defense.","hint":"Think about resisting an attack."},{"id":"P18","lesson":15,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Black to move: the kings face each other on e4 and e6, with one empty square between them. What does Black have to do, and does this decide who wins?","fen":"8/7p/4k3/8/4K3/8/P7/8 b - - 0 1","answer":0,"explanation":"The two kings face each other across e5, and Black must move first. This is called White having the opposition. Opposition may help White gain space, but the pawns and legal moves still determine the result.","hint":"Black is to move. Can a player skip a turn? Which king must leave its square?","options":["Black must move the king away from e6, but that alone does not determine the winner.","White must move its king immediately, so Black wins.","Black can leave its king on e6 and pass its turn.","White automatically wins, no matter where the pawns are."]},{"id":"P19","lesson":15,"kind":"mc","prompt":"White’s pawn stands on e5 and Black’s king on a8. Without other help, can the king catch the pawn?","fen":"k7/8/8/4P3/8/8/8/4K3 w - - 0 1","answer":1,"explanation":"No. White needs only e6, e7, e8=Q; Black’s king starts too far away to catch it.","hint":"Count pawn moves to promotion against the king’s route.","options":["Yes, the king controls e8 from a8.","No, the king is too far away.","Yes, because kings can move diagonally.","No, because the pawn has already promoted."]},{"id":"V15-a1","lesson":15,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In a king-and-pawn endgame, how can you use two kings facing each other with one empty square between them (called 'opposition')?","fen":"8/7p/4k3/8/4K3/8/P7/8 b - - 0 1","options":["Make the other king step aside so your king may enter useful squares.","Make your king move twice in a row.","Stop either player from moving their pawns.","Guarantee a win even if the other player can promote a pawn."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Opposition is about whose king must move away first. It often helps restrict the opposing king and gain access to useful squares. It does not guarantee a win.","hint":"Think about the fact that kings cannot stand next to each other."},{"id":"P24","lesson":16,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After losing a rapid game in the opening, which review notes are most helpful before turning on an engine?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","answer":1,"explanation":"Record the first surprise, a missed threat or tactical mistake, and what you considered playing. Then consult the engine.","hint":"The best notes preserve your reasoning rather than just the result.","options":["Only the final engine evaluation.","The first unfamiliar move, a missed threat, and your candidate plan.","The number of clicks and the time spent moving the queen.","Every legal move in the game, without comments."]},{"id":"V16-a1","lesson":16,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What should game review produce beyond an engine score?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["One specific habit to practice next time","A guarantee of a win","A list of every possible legal move","A new rating made by the engine"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A specific corrective habit makes analysis transferable.","hint":"Think about the next game."},{"id":"V16-a2","lesson":16,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When should you turn on the engine in a self-review workflow?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["After writing down your ideas and confusions","Before looking at the position","Only after winning","Never, because engines have no use"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Your thoughts provide learning context for engine evaluations.","hint":"Keep your decision record."},{"id":"V17-1","lesson":17,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A knight is attacked once and protected by no pieces. What is the immediate concern?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It may be captured without recapture","It automatically gives check","It must move to the edge","It prevents castling"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If the attacker can legally capture, the undefended knight can be lost.","hint":"Think about recaptures."},{"id":"V17-2","lesson":17,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When is counting attackers and defenders alone not enough?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["When a square is central","When a defending piece is pinned","When both players castled","When both queens remain"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pinned defenders may be unable to recapture, so count legal forcing replies.","hint":"Ask whether every defender can actually move."},{"id":"V17-t","lesson":17,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Before attacking a queen, your rook is left unprotected. Which habit should you use?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Scan your loose pieces first","Always ignore your rook","Capture your own rook","Only count pawn moves"],"answer":0,"explanation":"You need to consider loose material before starting your attack.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V18-1","lesson":18,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Before you play a queen move, what should you check first?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Whether it looks aggressive","Whether the opponent has a forcing reply","Whether it matches a famous opening","Whether you can move the same queen again"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Considering checks, captures and threats against your queen and king catches many blunders.","hint":"Think about the opponent’s turn."},{"id":"V18-2","lesson":18,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why compare at least two candidate moves?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The first legal move is always best","It makes the clock stop","An alternative may meet the same goal more safely","It prevents a draw by repetition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Alternatives reveal tactical problems and help you find safer improvements.","hint":"Your first idea is a hypothesis."},{"id":"V18-t","lesson":18,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your first move looks promising, but you see a counter-check. What next?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Calculate the forcing counter-check","Play immediately anyway","Resign","Ignore king safety"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A forcing counter-check can change your preferred move.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V19-1","lesson":19,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which action must be answered immediately under chess rules?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A pawn push","A discovered attack on a rook","A check to the king","A bishop developing to an open diagonal"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A player in check must legally remove the check.","hint":"One of these restricts legal replies."},{"id":"V19-2","lesson":19,"kind":"mc","prompt":"If your queen is attacked, which could be a valid response?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Capturing the attacker safely","Ignoring it to play an unrelated pawn move","Leaving the queen en prise always","Claiming a draw immediately"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A safe capture of the attacker can remove the threat without retreating.","hint":"Look for defense by forcing action."},{"id":"V19-t","lesson":19,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A side has an immediate capture but is also in check. What comes first?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Find a legal way out of check","Capture regardless","Offer a pawn","Make a neutral developing move"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Leaving the king in check is illegal.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V20-1","lesson":20,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A king is trapped behind its own pawns on the back row. When can an enemy rook or queen check be especially dangerous?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["When the king has no safe square to escape to.","When both bishops have already been traded.","When the attacker has two pawns on the same file.","When the queen moved before move five."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A back-rank mate occurs when a rook or queen checks a king trapped on its home row with no escape square.","hint":"Think about where the king could move after a check."},{"id":"V20-2","lesson":20,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which is a possible *preventive* idea against a back-rank mate?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Open a safe flight square for your king","Move every pawn toward the enemy king","Always sacrifice the rook","Refuse to castle in all positions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Creating luft can give the king an escape, provided the pawn move is safe.","hint":"Think about king mobility."},{"id":"V20-t","lesson":20,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A king has a safe escape square. Is every back-rank rook check mate?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["No; verify all king replies","Yes; any rook check is mate","Yes; pawns cannot move","Only in blitz"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An available safe flight square may refute the mating claim.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V21-1","lesson":21,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A rook is protecting a bishop and also stopping checkmate. What is the danger?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The rook may not be able to defend both threats at once (it is overloaded).","The rook has automatically been promoted.","The game is already a stalemate.","The rook can no longer move."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An overloaded piece has too many important defensive jobs. The attacker may force it to abandon one of them.","hint":"Imagine the rook is forced to defend one threat. What happens to the other?"},{"id":"V21-2","lesson":21,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why is capturing a defender sometimes useful?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It gives your opponent an extra piece","It opens a route to its protected target","It always wins instantly","It changes which side moves"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Eliminating the guard can expose a more valuable piece or critical mating square.","hint":"Focus on the defended target."},{"id":"V21-t","lesson":21,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A key defender is exchanged, exposing a hanging queen. What made the exchange useful?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It removed the guard","It created opposition","It promoted a pawn","It changed the time control"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Removing the defender exposes its target.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V22-1","lesson":22,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A sacrifice pulls a queen away from defending its king. Which motif is most relevant?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Deflection","Opposition","Underpromotion","Zugzwang"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The queen is redirected from a critical defensive duty.","hint":"What was the piece defending before it moved?"},{"id":"V22-2","lesson":22,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Placing a bishop between a rook and the rook’s defended pawn is an example of…","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A pawn race","Interference","Castling","A perpetual check"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Blocking the defender’s line is an interference tactic.","hint":"Picture the line from defender to target."},{"id":"V22-t","lesson":22,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A bishop can block a rook’s line to its defended queen. What should you evaluate?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Whether interference creates a forcing follow-up","Whether the bishop can jump pieces","Whether the king has castled twice","Whether the game uses increments"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Line interruption is only useful if the follow-up works.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V23-1","lesson":23,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the defining feature of a zwischenzug?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A forcing move inserted before an expected continuation","A pawn moving two squares","Any move before castling","The final move of an opening"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It changes the order of events with an immediate threat, often a check.","hint":"It interrupts the obvious sequence."},{"id":"V23-2","lesson":23,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After an opponent captures your bishop, what is worth checking before recapturing?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Whether you have a forcing check first","Whether you can offer a draw","Whether every pawn is defended","Whether you have moved the king before"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An intermediate check may improve your tactical result.","hint":"Look for moves the opponent must answer."},{"id":"V23-t","lesson":23,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You can recapture a pawn or check the enemy king first. What should you calculate?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The intermediate check and its response","Only the automatic recapture","Only pawn structures","Only your next opening"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Move order may alter the tactical sequence.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V24-1","lesson":24,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the main goal when surrounding the enemy king with several attacking pieces?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Take away all safe squares so the king cannot escape check.","Capture every pawn before giving check.","Promote a bishop into a queen.","Give exactly three checks in a row."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A 'mating net' is an arrangement of pieces that restricts the king's escape squares and can lead to checkmate.","hint":"Think about the squares to which the king could escape."},{"id":"V24-2","lesson":24,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why can a lone queen attack be dangerous for the attacker?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The queen has no legal moves","It may be chased while defenders develop","Queens cannot check kings alone","It forces stalemate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Premature solo queen attacks can lose time and allow the opponent to mobilize.","hint":"Consider coordination and tempo."},{"id":"V24-t","lesson":24,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your queen checks, but the opponent king can escape easily. What did you overlook?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Flight squares","Opening name","Move number","Material values"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A mating net requires restricting the king’s safe exits.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V25-1","lesson":25,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why is repeatedly moving the same piece in the opening sometimes a problem?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["You may use up moves without improving your position while the opponent develops several pieces.","Moving a piece twice automatically loses a pawn.","Moving a piece twice immediately ends the game.","Moving a piece twice means the king is in check."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each move is a turn you could use to improve a piece or defend a threat. A useful turn is called a tempo; spending extra turns without a good reason is called losing time or losing tempi.","hint":"Compare how many pieces each player has brought into the game."},{"id":"V25-2","lesson":25,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which is a sound reason to move an already-developed knight again?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It avoids losing the knight to a tactical threat","You always must move knights twice","You have no reason to castle","You want to imitate a grandmaster without checking"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Concrete threats sometimes override the rule against repeated moves.","hint":"Rules have tactical exceptions."},{"id":"V25-t","lesson":25,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You moved your bishop twice to avoid capture. Was that necessarily wrong?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["No; a concrete threat can justify it","Yes; moving pieces twice is illegal","Yes; all bishops must stay home","No; tempo never matters"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Threats can justify a second move.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V26-1","lesson":26,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why does c3 frequently support d4 in the Italian Game?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It prepares a central pawn advance","It castles immediately","It attacks the king on e8","It forces a queen trade"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The c-pawn can reinforce a later d4 pawn break.","hint":"Consider which pawn c3 supports."},{"id":"V26-2","lesson":26,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Before playing a central pawn break, which calculation matters most?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["What follows if the pawn is captured","Whether the pawn matches a memorized diagram","Whether you have played your h-pawn","Whether the move is aesthetically pleasing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The exchange sequence decides whether the break wins space or loses material.","hint":"Calculate the most forcing reply."},{"id":"V26-t","lesson":26,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your pawn break leaves a hanging bishop. What should you do?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Calculate the exchanges and loose bishop before committing","Push automatically","Ignore all threats","Trade kings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tactical safety controls pawn-break timing.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V27-1","lesson":27,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which move defines the Scotch Game after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["3.d4","3.h4","3.a3","3.g3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"3.d4 challenges the central e5 pawn immediately.","hint":"One choice pushes a central pawn."},{"id":"V27-2","lesson":27,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What does 3.Bb5 primarily pressure in the Ruy Lopez?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The c6 knight supporting e5","The h7 pawn","The rook on h8 directly","The king on e8 with check"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bb5 develops the bishop and pressures the e5 defender.","hint":"Think about what guards Black’s e5 pawn."},{"id":"V27-t","lesson":27,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You enjoy immediate central clashes. Which style might you explore first?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Scotch Game","A setup with only a-pawn pushes","An opening where White never plays a pawn","A forced endgame without pieces"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Scotch contests e5 with an early d4.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V28-1","lesson":28,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is White’s primary strategic goal with 1.d4 d5 2.c4?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Challenge Black’s d5 center","Force an immediate checkmate","Make castling illegal","Win the h7 pawn directly"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The c-pawn challenges Black’s central foothold on d5.","hint":"Focus on central pawn tension."},{"id":"V28-2","lesson":28,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which bishop development is typical of many London setups?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Bf4","Bh6","Ba6","Bxc8 on move two"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bf4 activates the c1 bishop before the e-pawn may block it.","hint":"Which bishop square keeps the piece outside its pawn chain?"},{"id":"V28-t","lesson":28,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent immediately attacks your London bishop. What should you do?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Respond to the real threat, not just a memorized setup","Play your next planned move regardless","Move the king for no reason","Offer a draw"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Opening systems do not remove the need for calculation.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V29-1","lesson":29,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which first move defines the French Defense against 1.e4?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["...e6","...g6","...a6","...h5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"1...e6 prepares to challenge the center with ...d5.","hint":"It prepares the d-pawn push."},{"id":"V29-2","lesson":29,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What makes 1...c5 (Sicilian) distinct from 1...e5?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It challenges the center asymmetrically","It blocks both bishops forever","It immediately checks White","It prevents White playing d4"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Black contests White’s d4 influence from the c-file rather than copying e5.","hint":"Consider which pawn Black moves."},{"id":"V29-t","lesson":29,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You are choosing a Black reply to e4. What matters besides its name?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Typical pawn structures and middlegame plans","Only internet popularity","Whether it guarantees a win","Whether it avoids all tactics"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plans and positions determine whether an opening suits you.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V30-1","lesson":30,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the defining central support move in the Slav Defense after 1.d4 d5 2.c4?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["...c6","...h6","...a5","...f5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"...c6 supports the d5 pawn without immediately closing the c8 bishop.","hint":"Which flank pawn reinforces d5?"},{"id":"V30-2","lesson":30,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a common strategic theme in the King’s Indian Defense?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Allow space and later strike back at the center","Force a queen trade on move two","Castle queenside in every game","Exchange all knights immediately"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Black often permits a broad White center and challenges it later.","hint":"Think counterplay against central space."},{"id":"V30-t","lesson":30,"kind":"mc","prompt":"An opening often concedes White central space. How should Black think?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Prepare a timely counterattack against the center","Assume the game is lost","Sacrifice everything","Avoid development forever"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A flexible counterattack can challenge a broad pawn center.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V31-1","lesson":31,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why can a rook often move more freely along a file (vertical column) with no pawns on it?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["No pawns block the rook's movement up and down the file.","The rook becomes worth nine points.","The rook may jump over other pieces.","No enemy piece can attack it there."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An open file is a vertical column with no pawns. Rooks can travel and attack along it without pawns obstructing their path.","hint":"Consider which pieces can block a rook on a column."},{"id":"V31-2","lesson":31,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a useful question before doubling rooks?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["What target or entry square will the pressure create?","How many moves will the queen make next?","Will my bishops become knights?","Will it end the game immediately?"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Doubling is worthwhile when it serves a plan, not merely because it looks coordinated.","hint":"Look for a purpose."},{"id":"V31-t","lesson":31,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your rook occupies an open file but has no entry square. What next?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Find a concrete target or plan","Double every rook automatically","Trade your king","Ignore opponent threats"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Activity is useful when it creates actual pressure.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V32-1","lesson":32,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which pawn is isolated?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A pawn with no friendly pawn on adjacent files","A pawn defended by two pawns","A pawn that has reached rank eight","A pawn directly next to its own king"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Without adjacent friendly pawns, that pawn lacks pawn support.","hint":"Look at the files on each side."},{"id":"V32-2","lesson":32,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why might a side accept an isolated queen’s pawn?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It may gain active piece play and open lines","It makes its king immune to check","It prevents all trades","It guarantees promotion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Activity and initiative can compensate for a long-term pawn weakness.","hint":"Look beyond the static pawn shape."},{"id":"V32-t","lesson":32,"kind":"mc","prompt":"An isolated pawn gives you open files and active pieces. How should you judge it?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Balance activity against the weakness","Assume any isolated pawn loses","Assume it always wins","Ignore pawn structure"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Dynamic compensation can offset pawn-structure weaknesses.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V33-1","lesson":33,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which is an important quality of a knight outpost?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Enemy pawns cannot easily chase it away","The knight is on the rim","The knight attacks its own king","It must be protected by a rook"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A durable outpost is difficult for enemy pawns to challenge.","hint":"Consider the enemy pawn structure."},{"id":"V33-2","lesson":33,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A bishop is blocked by many pawns of its own square color. What may help?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A well-timed pawn break","Moving the king immediately into the center","Trading both rooks at any cost","Ignoring its movement for the whole game"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing the pawn structure can open a useful diagonal for the bishop.","hint":"Think about opening lines."},{"id":"V33-t","lesson":33,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A knight looks centralized but an enemy pawn can immediately chase it away. Is it a durable outpost?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["No; a pawn can challenge it","Yes; all center squares are safe","Yes; knights cannot be chased","Only if both queens remain"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Durable outposts resist pawn challenges.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V34-1","lesson":34,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which is the strongest justification for sacrificing a bishop?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A calculated forced mate","It seems stylish","The bishop has moved twice","It happens in a famous opening trap"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A forced mate provides concrete justification; other sacrifices require clear compensation.","hint":"Look for forcing outcomes."},{"id":"V34-2","lesson":34,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What should you calculate before launching a king attack?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The opponent’s best defensive replies","Only the move you hope they play","Only your next pawn push","The number of videos on the opening"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sound attacks must survive the opponent’s strongest defense.","hint":"Assume your opponent finds a good response."},{"id":"V34-t","lesson":34,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You can sacrifice a rook for an attack but see a strong defense. What next?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Calculate that defense before sacrificing","Ignore it and sacrifice","Resign","Assume all sacrifices work"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sound sacrifices require concrete consequences.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V35-1","lesson":35,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why does king activity often increase in pawn endgames?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["With fewer attacking pieces, the king can safely help pawns","The king becomes a queen","Kings may move two squares","Checks are no longer legal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With major threats reduced, the king supports and attacks pawns directly.","hint":"Compare the number of remaining attackers."},{"id":"V35-2","lesson":35,"kind":"mc","prompt":"If two kings face each other with one empty square between them, and one king must move away first, does this alone guarantee a win?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["No. Pawn positions, turn order and possible pawn moves still matter.","Yes. Whichever king moves second always wins.","Only if a queen is on the board.","Only when both kings are on the a-file."],"answer":0,"explanation":"That king relationship is called opposition. It can be useful for taking space, but you still have to calculate whether a pawn can advance or promote.","hint":"Look at the pawns and other legal moves, not just the kings."},{"id":"V35-t","lesson":35,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Both players are racing to promote pawns, but your king is active. What should you calculate first?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["How many moves each pawn needs to promote, and whether a king can stop it.","Only how close your king is to the center.","The name of the opening used earlier.","Only how many moves have passed since move ten."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Count the moves (sometimes called tempi) each pawn needs and check whether a king can stop the promotion. An active king alone does not guarantee success.","hint":"Compare each pawn's distance from its promotion square."},{"id":"V36-1","lesson":36,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A pawn is trying to reach the last row and promote. What does the 'square rule' help you work out?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Whether the defending king can catch the pawn before it promotes.","Whether an opposing rook is pinned.","Which opening was played in the game.","How many moves a knight could make."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The square rule is a quick way to estimate whether a king can catch a passed pawn. You can also count the king's and pawn's moves directly.","hint":"Compare the king's distance with the pawn's remaining moves."},{"id":"V36-2","lesson":36,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why may a simple pawn-race count be wrong?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Checks and captures can change the sequence","Pawns cannot promote","The 50-move rule always ends first","Kings may pass their turn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Forcing moves and support can alter the apparent race.","hint":"Count forcing interruptions."},{"id":"V36-t","lesson":36,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent king is outside the pawn square, but has a checking capture available. What next?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Calculate the tactical exception","Declare promotion certain","Ignore capture","Pass your turn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Forcing moves can invalidate a simple square-rule impression.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V37-1","lesson":37,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When a pawn has a clear path toward promotion, where is a friendly rook often most useful?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Behind the advancing pawn, where the rook can support it.","Directly in front of the pawn, blocking its path.","Always beside your own king, even if far away.","On any other column without a clear purpose."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A passed pawn has no enemy pawns able to stop its advance on its own or adjacent files. A rook behind it often supports the pawn as it advances.","hint":"Which rook position keeps the pawn's forward path clear?"},{"id":"V37-2","lesson":37,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why is an extra pawn not always enough to win a rook endgame?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Active defense and checking can hold a draw","Rooks cannot give check","Pawn promotion is illegal with rooks","The side with more pawns always wins"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rook activity and king position can outweigh a small material edge.","hint":"Think about defensive checking."},{"id":"V37-t","lesson":37,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You have an extra pawn in a rook endgame but the opponent has active checks. What matters?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["King and rook activity alongside material","Material alone","Opening theory only","How fast you click"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Active defense can hold an extra-pawn ending.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V38-1","lesson":38,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which position is stalemate?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["A side to move has no legal moves and is not in check","A king is attacked and has no legal moves","A side has only its king left","Both sides have queens"],"answer":0,"explanation":"No legal move without check is stalemate; with check, it is checkmate.","hint":"Differentiate check from legal moves."},{"id":"V38-2","lesson":38,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why might trading pieces be useful when clearly ahead?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It can reduce counterplay and simplify conversion","It automatically creates mate","It always wins a pawn","It makes all remaining moves legal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Simplification can make it easier to turn a real material advantage into a win.","hint":"Think about your opponent’s remaining threats."},{"id":"V38-t","lesson":38,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You are up a queen but the opponent king has no legal move and is not in check. What is the result?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Draw by stalemate","Win by resignation","Forced checkmate","King promotion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A player without legal moves but not in check is stalemated.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V39-1","lesson":39,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why should you annotate your thinking before engine analysis?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["It preserves your actual decision process","It makes Stockfish stronger","It changes the legal moves","It guarantees a higher rating next game"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Your notes show the misconception to correct, rather than only the engine’s preferred move.","hint":"Remember your thought process."},{"id":"V39-2","lesson":39,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What makes a useful takeaway from a review?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["One repeatable decision habit","The exact engine score only","A promise to never lose again","Memorizing every move in the game"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A concrete habit transfers to future decisions more reliably than a raw evaluation.","hint":"What could you do differently next game?"},{"id":"V39-t","lesson":39,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Stockfish says your move was inferior. What should you write in your notes?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The threat or idea you missed","Only an exclamation mark","That engines never err","That a rating point was stolen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Record the transferable mistake.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."},{"id":"V40-1","lesson":40,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When is it most useful to evaluate a calculated line?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["After considering the opponent’s strongest response","Immediately after your first move only","After copying engine notation with no thought","Only after the game ends"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The result depends on the full relevant sequence, not just your first idea.","hint":"Imagine the opponent defending well."},{"id":"V40-2","lesson":40,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a helpful note after missing a tactical puzzle?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["The specific pattern you overlooked","The number of pieces on the starting board","Your favorite opening name","That every engine score is perfect"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Naming the missed motif helps you recognize it in a new position.","hint":"Identify the transferable cause."},{"id":"V40-t","lesson":40,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You solved a puzzle only after two hints. Should that count as fully mastered?","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","options":["Not yet; repeat it without hints later","Yes; any solved attempt is mastery","No; you must restart chess entirely","Only if the puzzle is a fork"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Independent recall is a better indicator of mastery.","hint":"Identify the real tactical or strategic cause before deciding."}]};